00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:26.710
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
So today, we're

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.360
moving along chronologically.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.820
And today, we're going to look
at two films from the 1920s.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.403
And both of these
films were extremely

00:00:32.403 --> 00:00:35.050
influential on later
documentary filmmakers, which

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.560
is why we want to look at them.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.650
But they have extremely
different sensibilities

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:41.080
and extremely different styles.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.720
And they also have
extremely different visions

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.880
of what they want to do
with this new emerging

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.640
camera film technology.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.520
And they also are in
dialogue with science

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.670
in different kinds of ways.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.410
And we talked some about
science films before,

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:55.952
and that's something
that we're going

00:00:55.952 --> 00:00:58.030
to draw out in both of these--

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.757
thinking about in relation
to both of these filmmakers.

00:01:00.757 --> 00:01:02.590
So as I mentioned in
the last class, the way

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:04.423
that we do these Wednesday
night classes is,

00:01:04.423 --> 00:01:06.702
generally, I introduce
the films that we're

00:01:06.702 --> 00:01:09.160
going to be talking about, then
we watch part of the films,

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.400
and then we have group
discussion about it afterwards.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.020
Because we don't
have enough time,

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.600
unfortunately, we can't
watch the documentaries

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.170
in their entirety, but we do
have copies of most things

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:21.730
over in the DV lab office.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.380
And for a lot of the films,
we have three copies of each.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.740
So if you want to borrow
DVDs and watch the rest of it

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.270
at some point if
you have a chance,

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:31.400
you can also get them out.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.060
Take them out with
Dave in addition

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.967
to getting camera
equipment from him.

00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:38.800
OK, so the first film
we're going to look at

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.342
is a film called
Nanook of the North.

00:01:40.342 --> 00:01:42.940
I don't know how many
have ever heard of Nanook.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.075
It's quite a famous
documentary film.

00:01:45.075 --> 00:01:46.450
And as you know
from the reading,

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:47.742
it was made by Robert Flaherty.

00:01:47.742 --> 00:01:49.570
It came out in 1922.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:53.080
And this film was made about
Inuit peoples who are often

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:56.470
referred to historically
by Europeans and Americans

00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:02.560
as Eskimo, people who group
that lived historically

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.500
as well as in the contemporary
northern Canada and the Arctic.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.250
And the film ostensibly tracks
the life of an Eskimo family

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:13.900
and their struggles to survive
in the harsh Arctic wilderness.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.260
And in thinking about the
relationship between science

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.390
and documentary in
this class, there's

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:21.910
a couple of reasons
why Nanook of the North

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:23.200
is important to think about.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.300
First of all, because it's
often credited as being

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.370
the first documentary film.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:30.550
And as we talked about in
the last class, the term

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:34.110
documentary itself wasn't
really coined until the 1930s.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.610
So you didn't really, at this
point, it was still too early,

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.260
you didn't really
have a distinct genre

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:39.680
of documentary film.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.470
But for documentary
film theorists,

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:44.920
when they go back and look at
the history of various kinds

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.005
of films, they often
position Nanook

00:02:47.005 --> 00:02:50.860
of the North as being the first
documentary film historically.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.248
And it's also been influential
among anthropologists

00:02:54.248 --> 00:02:56.290
in the social sciences
because many people saw it

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.810
as a precursor to
ethnographic film.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.507
And if you're not sure about
anthropology or ethnographic

00:03:01.507 --> 00:03:04.090
film, we're going to be talking
about that a couple of classes

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:05.430
later down the line.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:10.340
So there'll be more discussion
about that coming up.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.950
So I mentioned in
the last class how,

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:13.908
from the very beginning,
from the time that you

00:03:13.908 --> 00:03:15.850
had the Lumiere
brothers inventing

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:20.530
their camera in the 1890s,
the early history of the film

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:24.220
were very international
in origin.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:25.740
So we talked about
how the Lumieres,

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.970
they immediately
had their operators

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:31.750
going out at different parts of
the world, taking these films,

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:33.110
showing them to audiences.

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:34.490
So from the very,
very beginning,

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:37.060
it was a very international--

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:38.807
it had very international scope.

00:03:38.807 --> 00:03:40.390
And I also mentioned
in the last class

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:41.800
that you have to keep
in mind this was also

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:42.890
the colonial period.

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:47.530
So this was really the height
of European colonialism.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:51.528
1/3 of the landmass of the
globe during this period

00:03:51.528 --> 00:03:53.320
was controlled by the
British in some form,

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:57.070
either through direct colonies
or indirect protectorates.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.590
Some people argue that 1/4
of the world's population

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:01.540
were under some form
of British dominion.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:06.550
So between the various
major European powers--

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:10.690
and the US also had its
hands in some of this,

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.892
but it was very much a
colonial international terrain.

00:04:14.892 --> 00:04:17.350
And so you have to think about
the dissemination of cameras

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:19.269
and early films in
that kind of context

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:20.649
as they moved internationally.

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:22.774
And you also have to think
in the context of the US

00:04:22.774 --> 00:04:24.670
and Canada, you
also had internally

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:26.380
colonized people
like Native Americans

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:29.800
or the Inuit in Arctic Canada.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.020
And I don't know how
many of you have talked

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.950
about this in basic
history classes elsewhere,

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:37.600
but in the 19th
and 20th centuries

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.520
you might know that
ideas of social evolution

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:40.480
were very common.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.200
Any sense of what those ideas
of social evolution were about?

00:04:50.635 --> 00:04:52.130
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:54.950
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, I
mean, some people refer to it

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:55.970
as social Darwinism.

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:58.010
I mean, that was a later strand.

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:01.490
It also had earlier
historical origins.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:04.880
But, basically the idea
in much of 19th century

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.070
European and American
thought that you

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:10.250
could rank societies
hierarchically,

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.062
that societies were ranked.

00:05:12.062 --> 00:05:13.520
There were some
societies that were

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.760
seen as being more primitive
and more traditional.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:18.380
And they were seen
as being ranked.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.900
And the groups that were
seen as being the most

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.550
civilized, or modern,
or progressive

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:26.030
were European countries.

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:28.310
And Americans thought, of
course, Americans were.

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:30.518
But it was very much this
idea that somehow societies

00:05:30.518 --> 00:05:32.727
could be ranked in a certain
kind of way and the idea

00:05:32.727 --> 00:05:34.400
that societies would
evolve or transform

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.750
from being more primitive
or traditional to being

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:43.630
more modern.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:45.330
So these kinds of
stereotypes and ideas

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:47.320
were very pervasive
during this period.

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:49.170
And many of these
stereotypes then

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:52.260
about non-western societies
were extremely negative.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.600
And partly, that came out
of the colonial context.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.298
It was part of
the understandings

00:05:57.298 --> 00:05:59.340
of this hierarchical
relationships between people

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:00.884
in different parts of the world.

00:06:00.884 --> 00:06:04.050
But in addition to the
overtly negative stereotypes

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.150
of non-western people, you
also had a certain kind

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:12.120
of romantic Rousseauian ideas
of the noble primitive savage

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.030
that certain
primitive peoples were

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.790
seen as kind of
a romantic light,

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:21.840
as being closer to nature,
as being potentially more

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:22.680
authentic.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.860
So there was this kind of
countercurrent in European

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:29.050
and American thought where at
least some people would argue,

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.220
well, modern society
is corrupting

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:35.760
and these non-western
more, quote-unquote,

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.640
"primitive peoples" are
somehow more authentic,

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.910
they haven't been corrupted
by modern contemporary life.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:45.270
And so many people have talked
about Nanook of the North

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:48.480
as being a film that came
out of this romantic,

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.000
this romantic
primitive tradition.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.550
It was a wildly popular film.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:56.190
And we talked in the last class
about how the early science

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:58.320
films were also part of
popular entertainment.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:02.610
They were shown in movie
theaters with Vaudeville shows.

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:07.740
So entertainment was very much a
part of the early film terrain.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:11.170
And Nanook as entertainment
was wildly successful.

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:12.515
There were press kits.

00:07:12.515 --> 00:07:13.890
And I actually--
I can't remember

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:15.900
whether I had this online, but
for those of you interested,

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:16.980
I'll pass this around.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.930
This is some of the
early press kits

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:22.830
that when Nanook was
being shown in theaters,

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:26.977
they had all these ideas for
shopkeepers about what you

00:07:26.977 --> 00:07:29.310
should do to publicize it,
and all these ideas about how

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:30.893
you should build
igloos in your lobby,

00:07:30.893 --> 00:07:32.610
and put around fake snow.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:36.660
And so anyway, the press
the press kit from the 1920s

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.040
is quite interesting
about ideas about how

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:40.380
Nanook should be publicized.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:44.610
So Nanook, it turned in
kind of a pop culture craze.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:50.430
All these commercial tie ins,
Eskimo pies became a big item

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:51.135
in pop culture.

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:57.180
So one of the questions
to think about then was,

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:00.090
why was this kind of Romantic
Primitivism so popular

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:01.090
at the time?

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:04.673
And Anna Grimshaw
is one author who's

00:08:04.673 --> 00:08:06.090
argued that in
order to understand

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:08.123
the popularity of
films like Nanook

00:08:08.123 --> 00:08:09.540
and these kinds
of ideas, you have

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:13.200
to think about the US and Europe
in the context of the aftermath

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.810
of World War I.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:18.360
And what she argued was
that the First World

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:20.610
War was so deeply traumatic
for so many people

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:25.680
that it caused them to
question the 19th century

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.850
strong faith in ideas of
science and technology

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:32.490
as always leading to
greater rationality.

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:35.549
And for a lot of people,
those kinds of ideas

00:08:35.549 --> 00:08:39.330
that society was just evolving
in this kind of upward way,

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:42.090
and everything was
becoming more progressive,

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:44.039
and that science and
technology were going

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:46.300
to bring only wonderful things.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:48.360
The first World War was
a very strong challenge

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.310
for a lot of people.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:52.770
And she argues that there
were a couple of responses

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:55.650
to this, that surrealism, if you
think about other kinds of art

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.510
movements like
surrealism and Dadaism

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:00.990
were one kind of response,
because the surrealist ideas

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:03.990
of breaking down rationality
and focusing on dream states,

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:06.690
for example, that was
one kind of response

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:08.100
to that kind of disillusionment.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:09.840
And she argues that
another response was

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.650
a certain kind of romantic
primitivism, this idea, then,

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:17.250
that, well, these supposedly
primitive peoples were leading

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:19.320
more simple authentic
lives and that they

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.900
didn't have to worry about
the horrors of modern warfare

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:24.930
or the alienation
of modern life.

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:29.250
So there was this fascination
at that particular historical

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:31.455
moment with these
kinds of groups.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.960
So in addition to
focusing on what

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:40.920
were thought of at the time
as being primitive peoples,

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.720
there were also the
romantic tradition

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:46.200
has historically focused
very strongly on nature.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:48.330
And there was often
expressed a kind

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:52.620
of longing for an untamed
nature and wild spaces,

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:55.980
which was associated with the
fact that many of those spaces

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.920
were being transformed because
of colonialism or because

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:00.340
of economic expansion.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:04.830
So there was often a kind of
nostalgia for certain kinds

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:05.980
of landscapes.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:09.210
So Nanook was a film that
brought both of those trends

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:11.850
together, an interest
in a certain kind

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:14.040
of romantic primitivism
that was also

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:15.480
tied to certain
kinds of nostalgia

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:18.840
for certain kinds of
natural landscapes.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:20.790
And you've read a
bit in the readings

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:23.417
about Flaherty's
background, the filmmaker.

00:10:23.417 --> 00:10:25.500
You know that he was the
son of a mining engineer,

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:28.200
he grew up in northern Michigan.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:31.290
He was very kind of outdoorsy
type ever since he was a kid.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:33.450
And he started out in
the north because he was

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:36.870
searching for mining deposits.

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:40.260
And then, he increasingly
began working on film projects

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:43.080
with the Inuit.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:44.580
You might have read
about how he had

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:48.180
an early version of the film
that ended up being set on fire

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:49.240
and destroyed.

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:54.990
And he ended up going back
and reworking it completely.

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:57.750
But anyway, we can talk
more about Flaherty

00:10:57.750 --> 00:10:59.250
after you've seen
a bit of the film.

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:00.625
So just a couple
of things I want

00:11:00.625 --> 00:11:02.490
you to be thinking
about as we watch

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:04.273
a bit of Nanook of the North.

00:11:04.273 --> 00:11:06.690
The question of, why was it
so appealing to so many people

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:07.720
at the time?

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:10.560
Why is this considered to be
a classic, a classic film?

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:16.800
And it still is often on the
list of top films ever made,

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:18.473
you'll find Nanook.

00:11:18.473 --> 00:11:19.890
The second question
to think about

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:23.100
is, how is it constructed as
a film, as a documentary film?

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:26.190
How is it put together,
how is it made?

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:28.920
And the third
question is, what are

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:33.090
the kinds of representations
at work in the film about Inuit

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:35.215
as a group?

00:11:35.215 --> 00:11:37.590
So just a few things to be
thinking about as we watch it.

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:41.100
We're going to watch about
20 minutes to half an hour

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.227
of the film.

00:11:43.227 --> 00:11:44.225
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:11:44.225 --> 00:11:47.219
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:39:30.688 --> 00:39:32.420
[END PLAYBACK]

00:39:32.420 --> 00:39:32.920
OK.

00:39:32.920 --> 00:39:33.715
Reactions?

00:39:33.715 --> 00:39:34.215
Thoughts?

00:39:37.653 --> 00:39:38.570
What do you all think?

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:46.980
AUDIENCE: For something made
in the early 1990s, it wasn't--

00:39:46.980 --> 00:39:48.105
it was pretty good.

00:39:48.105 --> 00:39:49.483
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:49.483 --> 00:39:50.400
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it is.

00:39:50.400 --> 00:39:52.352
It kind of holds up, doesn't it?

00:39:52.352 --> 00:39:53.310
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

00:39:53.310 --> 00:39:54.560
So what did you like about it?

00:39:58.145 --> 00:39:59.115
AUDIENCE: I don't know.

00:39:59.115 --> 00:40:04.260
I guess it just had that
old movie feel to it.

00:40:04.260 --> 00:40:08.580
It reminded me of the
last STS class I was in,

00:40:08.580 --> 00:40:11.908
we watched a few clips from
Charlie Chaplin's Modern Times.

00:40:11.908 --> 00:40:13.450
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Yeah, that's great.

00:40:13.450 --> 00:40:15.570
AUDIENCE: And it
just reminded me

00:40:15.570 --> 00:40:17.950
of, like, that
similar type of movie.

00:40:17.950 --> 00:40:21.660
Just has a feel good type.

00:40:21.660 --> 00:40:25.025
I don't know how to
express it personally.

00:40:25.025 --> 00:40:26.400
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Other thoughts?

00:40:26.400 --> 00:40:26.900
Comments?

00:40:26.900 --> 00:40:28.525
AUDIENCE: It's cool,
because it's stuff

00:40:28.525 --> 00:40:30.960
like I've never seen, like,
as far as, like, the igloo.

00:40:30.960 --> 00:40:32.918
I mean, even though
nowadays you could probably

00:40:32.918 --> 00:40:34.280
go out there a lot easier.

00:40:34.280 --> 00:40:37.987
But yeah, like they don't
show that type of stuff

00:40:37.987 --> 00:40:39.570
because it's more,
like, not following

00:40:39.570 --> 00:40:43.008
one person in modern films
about visiting places.

00:40:43.008 --> 00:40:44.830
So yeah.

00:40:44.830 --> 00:40:46.580
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah,
and, I mean, we're

00:40:46.580 --> 00:40:48.122
going to be talking
particularly when

00:40:48.122 --> 00:40:49.670
we look at Vertov's
film, which is

00:40:49.670 --> 00:40:53.480
a film very much about modern
technology at the time.

00:40:53.480 --> 00:40:56.300
So again, thinking
about Flaherty

00:40:56.300 --> 00:40:58.395
as somebody who's partly
reacting against that.

00:40:58.395 --> 00:41:00.020
And so here is like
a real appreciation

00:41:00.020 --> 00:41:01.062
with that igloo building.

00:41:01.062 --> 00:41:03.410
It's like, ah, look at
here's a technology that

00:41:03.410 --> 00:41:08.050
wouldn't be considered modern
by contemporary Southerners

00:41:08.050 --> 00:41:10.760
as Inuit people would
refer to people down here.

00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:12.440
But isn't this
fascinating watching

00:41:12.440 --> 00:41:13.815
the way that they
put it together

00:41:13.815 --> 00:41:16.400
and the amount of ingenuity
that goes into doing it?

00:41:16.400 --> 00:41:19.610
So it's about technology
in an interesting way,

00:41:19.610 --> 00:41:22.480
but different than some
of the focus on technology

00:41:22.480 --> 00:41:24.230
that we'll see in some
of the other films.

00:41:24.230 --> 00:41:25.430
Jessica, you had your hand up?

00:41:25.430 --> 00:41:27.013
AUDIENCE: Oh, yeah,
I was going to say

00:41:27.013 --> 00:41:28.905
what I found interesting,
or at least what

00:41:28.905 --> 00:41:31.280
got me really interested in
the film was that it's, like,

00:41:31.280 --> 00:41:36.620
a totally different way of
life, different from the viewers

00:41:36.620 --> 00:41:41.510
in that day when it was
published or shown and now.

00:41:41.510 --> 00:41:45.290
I think that was what
really fascinated me.

00:41:45.290 --> 00:41:47.990
And it was just interesting
how I think how they

00:41:47.990 --> 00:41:50.210
interacted with the filmer.

00:41:50.210 --> 00:41:53.688
Like, sometimes they look at
the camera and sometimes they--

00:41:53.688 --> 00:41:54.230
I don't know.

00:41:54.230 --> 00:41:55.940
Sometimes it seemed scripted.

00:41:55.940 --> 00:42:00.140
Like, the fishing part
where he's, like, there's

00:42:00.140 --> 00:42:01.320
all these different angles.

00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:03.590
And I was like, what, like,
how is he there, like,

00:42:03.590 --> 00:42:04.495
all the time?

00:42:04.495 --> 00:42:05.870
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Yeah, so that's

00:42:05.870 --> 00:42:07.790
an important point that
we should go back to

00:42:07.790 --> 00:42:09.240
is how the piece
was put together.

00:42:09.240 --> 00:42:10.220
Maybe we could talk
about the film more

00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:11.480
generally and then go
back to those questions

00:42:11.480 --> 00:42:12.890
about how it was put together.

00:42:12.890 --> 00:42:15.320
Oh, and I should just say the
parts that you didn't see,

00:42:15.320 --> 00:42:17.210
for any of you who get a
chance to watch the rest of it,

00:42:17.210 --> 00:42:19.460
there's a number of really
interesting hunting scenes.

00:42:19.460 --> 00:42:21.050
So there's more
with them fishing

00:42:21.050 --> 00:42:24.860
and then there's also
a very famous scene

00:42:24.860 --> 00:42:28.790
with a seal trying to
hook a seal through an ice

00:42:28.790 --> 00:42:31.520
hole, a very famous
walrus hunting scene.

00:42:31.520 --> 00:42:32.880
So a lot of it's hunting.

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:35.120
And then, there's also
a big storm comes up

00:42:35.120 --> 00:42:36.690
and they have to get to safety.

00:42:36.690 --> 00:42:42.180
And so basically it's
about day-to-day survival.

00:42:42.180 --> 00:42:44.390
So that's the pieces
that you didn't see.

00:42:44.390 --> 00:42:47.210
But then, what you started
out by saying, the fascination

00:42:47.210 --> 00:42:49.993
with this way of life,
I mean, one thing

00:42:49.993 --> 00:42:51.410
to keep in mind
at the time period

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:53.510
and it's still true
still true today,

00:42:53.510 --> 00:42:55.430
that one of the
things that people

00:42:55.430 --> 00:42:57.560
were interested in with
the very early films

00:42:57.560 --> 00:43:04.160
was using it as a way
to explore or gain

00:43:04.160 --> 00:43:09.080
some knowledge, however
superficial or deep, of people

00:43:09.080 --> 00:43:10.260
in other places.

00:43:10.260 --> 00:43:13.010
So you can think of it
during a time period

00:43:13.010 --> 00:43:17.390
when contemporary tourism in
with the ease which we do it

00:43:17.390 --> 00:43:19.560
was obviously not an
option for many people.

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:21.200
So a fascination
with film is a way

00:43:21.200 --> 00:43:25.460
to get some sense
of what life was

00:43:25.460 --> 00:43:28.910
like in different places being
a primary interest for people

00:43:28.910 --> 00:43:30.278
in early cinema.

00:43:30.278 --> 00:43:32.070
Other-- Matt, I think
you had your hand up.

00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:33.830
AUDIENCE: I found
it interesting,

00:43:33.830 --> 00:43:36.020
obviously in modern day
you had a narrator going on

00:43:36.020 --> 00:43:38.510
while they're doing
the action, but you

00:43:38.510 --> 00:43:41.332
had to preface it as much as you
could but as short as he could.

00:43:41.332 --> 00:43:42.290
CHRISTINE WALLEY: What?

00:43:42.290 --> 00:43:42.920
I'm sorry?

00:43:42.920 --> 00:43:44.960
AUDIENCE: He had the
preface every scene as much

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:46.790
as you could, but in
short of the sentences

00:43:46.790 --> 00:43:50.270
as he could since you can't
just fill screen with two

00:43:50.270 --> 00:43:51.740
paragraphs worth of writing.

00:43:51.740 --> 00:43:52.740
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Right.

00:43:52.740 --> 00:43:57.090
So because it's a silent film
and there's no narration,

00:43:57.090 --> 00:43:58.370
there's no dialogue.

00:43:58.370 --> 00:44:00.800
So to try to explain
things for audiences

00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:03.620
who wouldn't be familiar, these
really brief intro titles.

00:44:06.150 --> 00:44:06.650
Yeah.

00:44:06.650 --> 00:44:11.630
And quite evocative
for a popular audience,

00:44:11.630 --> 00:44:15.440
many of the titles in
terms of picking interest.

00:44:15.440 --> 00:44:16.880
AUDIENCE: I think
definitely what

00:44:16.880 --> 00:44:21.745
you were saying about how
different these people look

00:44:21.745 --> 00:44:23.120
or have been
represented, I think

00:44:23.120 --> 00:44:25.037
there is really this
interplay of similarities

00:44:25.037 --> 00:44:25.880
and differences.

00:44:25.880 --> 00:44:29.090
What's most striking about
the film, like, he has--

00:44:29.090 --> 00:44:30.590
Flaherty has made
all the efforts

00:44:30.590 --> 00:44:36.890
to show them as very different,
for the environment they live

00:44:36.890 --> 00:44:41.510
is nothing, the audience have
seen or can even imagine,

00:44:41.510 --> 00:44:43.820
they're completely unfamiliar
with the gramophone

00:44:43.820 --> 00:44:45.770
or at least they're
represented to be so.

00:44:45.770 --> 00:44:47.630
There is, like, absolutely no--

00:44:47.630 --> 00:44:50.030
and yet at the
same time, they're

00:44:50.030 --> 00:44:53.040
very similar to kind of a
European nuclear family.

00:44:53.040 --> 00:44:56.860
The wife is beautifully
cleaning the window,

00:44:56.860 --> 00:45:00.200
and the kids are
given their medicine,

00:45:00.200 --> 00:45:02.363
and they play while
the father works.

00:45:02.363 --> 00:45:04.280
So there is also something
kind of reassuring.

00:45:04.280 --> 00:45:07.070
Like, these people
are so different

00:45:07.070 --> 00:45:09.262
and yet they are
they're like us.

00:45:09.262 --> 00:45:10.220
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

00:45:10.220 --> 00:45:12.210
So let's so let's
talk about this.

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:14.293
So one of the questions I
asked you to think about

00:45:14.293 --> 00:45:19.460
is representations of the
Inuit in this film and also

00:45:19.460 --> 00:45:20.960
the question about
why it would have

00:45:20.960 --> 00:45:22.110
been appealing at the time.

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:24.780
And so what you're saying
goes a bit to both of those.

00:45:24.780 --> 00:45:29.330
But can we draw
that out a bit more?

00:45:29.330 --> 00:45:32.570
So what was the
image of the Inuit

00:45:32.570 --> 00:45:35.288
that Flaherty was trying to
construct through this film?

00:45:35.288 --> 00:45:37.040
Go.

00:45:37.040 --> 00:45:40.220
AUDIENCE: I think that,
I mean, a big part of it

00:45:40.220 --> 00:45:41.150
was, like, the music.

00:45:41.150 --> 00:45:45.160
But like, it made the
Inuit lifestyle seem very,

00:45:45.160 --> 00:45:47.860
they said, like, they were
happy go lucky, even though

00:45:47.860 --> 00:45:49.810
like their lifestyle is--

00:45:49.810 --> 00:45:54.310
like, building an igloo is
not, like, fun for any means,

00:45:54.310 --> 00:45:55.420
I'm sure.

00:45:55.420 --> 00:45:58.930
So, like, they are portrayed
as, like, it almost

00:45:58.930 --> 00:46:02.110
seems kind of like storybook
with, like, the prefaces,

00:46:02.110 --> 00:46:04.970
and then it's, like, the
parents are building the igloo

00:46:04.970 --> 00:46:06.220
and then the kids are playing.

00:46:06.220 --> 00:46:09.370
Like, everything seems,
like, easy and fun.

00:46:09.370 --> 00:46:14.770
But I guess the
dangers that they

00:46:14.770 --> 00:46:18.490
face in a day-to-day
lifestyle, like the starvation,

00:46:18.490 --> 00:46:21.162
isn't really brought
across in the film.

00:46:21.162 --> 00:46:22.120
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

00:46:22.120 --> 00:46:23.560
If we had seen the
hunting scenes,

00:46:23.560 --> 00:46:25.102
you would have got
a bit more of that

00:46:25.102 --> 00:46:27.430
because the walrus
scene is shown to be

00:46:27.430 --> 00:46:31.420
a very dangerous enterprise.

00:46:31.420 --> 00:46:34.365
But anyway, but your
larger point being,

00:46:34.365 --> 00:46:35.740
again, these
questions they have,

00:46:35.740 --> 00:46:39.640
how are they being represented?

00:46:39.640 --> 00:46:46.180
Any thought building on
these questions, comments?

00:46:46.180 --> 00:46:49.570
AUDIENCE: I mean the
childlike, everything

00:46:49.570 --> 00:46:53.950
is kind of cute, ingenious,
children, even the starting

00:46:53.950 --> 00:46:57.250
scene when all of them
supposedly come out

00:46:57.250 --> 00:47:00.100
of the tiny boat
and they're kind

00:47:00.100 --> 00:47:04.360
of smiling in this clumsy
way, and there is, like,

00:47:04.360 --> 00:47:09.047
no self-reflection, there
is no quotes from them,

00:47:09.047 --> 00:47:10.630
I'm sure they
participate in the film,

00:47:10.630 --> 00:47:13.602
but they're not presented as
being engaged in the film.

00:47:13.602 --> 00:47:14.560
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:16.660
So there's definitely
a sense of humor

00:47:16.660 --> 00:47:19.120
in a lot of the film, which
we can think of that as part

00:47:19.120 --> 00:47:20.470
of the reason why it was
appealing at the time,

00:47:20.470 --> 00:47:22.240
because there's parts are
quite funny in addition

00:47:22.240 --> 00:47:24.040
to the insanely cute
parts with the kids.

00:47:24.040 --> 00:47:25.900
There's actually some
incredibly cute stuff

00:47:25.900 --> 00:47:28.190
that you didn't see
of some of the kids.

00:47:28.190 --> 00:47:30.280
They make these
little snow animals

00:47:30.280 --> 00:47:32.020
and then they have
these little toy bow

00:47:32.020 --> 00:47:33.340
and arrows that they're
shooting them with.

00:47:33.340 --> 00:47:34.757
It's just, like,
the cutest thing.

00:47:34.757 --> 00:47:37.285
Everybody goes, like, aw
when I show it in my classes.

00:47:40.270 --> 00:47:47.590
So there's the use
of humor, there's

00:47:47.590 --> 00:47:52.930
cute images of kids as a way
to create a sense of appeal

00:47:52.930 --> 00:47:55.660
as part of it.

00:47:55.660 --> 00:47:58.795
But again, these larger
questions that you're raising,

00:47:58.795 --> 00:48:01.420
I mean-- and Sean mentioned this
also with this the description

00:48:01.420 --> 00:48:03.005
of them as
happy-go-lucky Eskimos--

00:48:05.828 --> 00:48:07.870
what's potentially being
left out of the picture?

00:48:07.870 --> 00:48:08.380
[INAUDIBLE]

00:48:08.380 --> 00:48:10.840
AUDIENCE: I mean, actually, my
impression was of the opposite

00:48:10.840 --> 00:48:12.882
that, I don't know, I
think it's very subjective.

00:48:12.882 --> 00:48:16.000
But I thought, building
up on your idea

00:48:16.000 --> 00:48:17.800
that social evolution
in the hierarchy,

00:48:17.800 --> 00:48:20.680
I think it was more
trying to show how we

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:22.150
think of the primitive people.

00:48:22.150 --> 00:48:23.950
I think we have
this miserable life.

00:48:23.950 --> 00:48:27.070
They have to starve, they don't
have modern modern technology,

00:48:27.070 --> 00:48:30.140
so they have to put up
with really awful stuff.

00:48:30.140 --> 00:48:32.950
But then, we see that
they're happy children.

00:48:32.950 --> 00:48:35.770
They still live even though
they have to go hunting.

00:48:35.770 --> 00:48:37.338
But they still,
they're are people.

00:48:37.338 --> 00:48:39.880
They have a happy life and they
enjoy their life just as much

00:48:39.880 --> 00:48:42.910
as we do with all the war,
and modernity, and technology

00:48:42.910 --> 00:48:44.244
that they have.

00:48:44.244 --> 00:48:45.520
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Serena,
do you have a [INAUDIBLE]??

00:48:45.520 --> 00:48:47.920
AUDIENCE: Yeah, no, I think also
it shows how hard working they

00:48:47.920 --> 00:48:49.550
really are, because all
this stuff they're doing,

00:48:49.550 --> 00:48:51.597
it's something that we
wouldn't know how to do.

00:48:51.597 --> 00:48:53.930
Like, if we went out there,
we couldn't do it ourselves.

00:48:53.930 --> 00:48:55.305
And so even though
it's different

00:48:55.305 --> 00:48:57.880
and, like, it might not
be as complex as something

00:48:57.880 --> 00:49:00.340
that we think now is
modern technology,

00:49:00.340 --> 00:49:02.892
it's like, they're working
really hard, which is kind of--

00:49:02.892 --> 00:49:03.850
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

00:49:03.850 --> 00:49:05.740
So I mean, what a lot
of you are building on,

00:49:05.740 --> 00:49:08.120
I mean, there's kind of two
contradictory things going on

00:49:08.120 --> 00:49:08.620
here.

00:49:08.620 --> 00:49:12.340
I mean, in line with the kind
of romantic primitivist ideas

00:49:12.340 --> 00:49:16.360
at the time, there is a
certain portrayal of Inuit

00:49:16.360 --> 00:49:18.520
where you don't see much--

00:49:18.520 --> 00:49:20.590
you don't see their
interaction with Europeans.

00:49:20.590 --> 00:49:22.660
I mean, you did see in the part
that you saw the interaction

00:49:22.660 --> 00:49:23.500
with the trader.

00:49:23.500 --> 00:49:26.770
But that's the only
bit in the whole film

00:49:26.770 --> 00:49:28.990
that actually shows
interactions with the Europeans.

00:49:28.990 --> 00:49:34.180
And there's a very long
history in this region of--

00:49:34.180 --> 00:49:37.450
whether Russians, or
Canadians, or whoever coming up

00:49:37.450 --> 00:49:40.930
and having very problematic
power relationships with Inuit

00:49:40.930 --> 00:49:42.880
people up in that region,
and you don't really

00:49:42.880 --> 00:49:45.430
get that other than the
short scene with the trader

00:49:45.430 --> 00:49:48.135
and where it's all
presented as funny and cute.

00:49:48.135 --> 00:49:50.260
So you don't have a sense
of the historical context

00:49:50.260 --> 00:49:52.090
in which people are living.

00:49:52.090 --> 00:49:53.545
For those of you--
and I know you

00:49:53.545 --> 00:49:55.960
have done different sets of
reading, because most of you

00:49:55.960 --> 00:49:56.980
just did the required reading.

00:49:56.980 --> 00:49:58.720
And Catarina did the
recommended reading

00:49:58.720 --> 00:50:01.450
where she has a little
bit of extra added info.

00:50:01.450 --> 00:50:03.460
But there are certain
kinds of the things

00:50:03.460 --> 00:50:04.715
that got left out of the film.

00:50:04.715 --> 00:50:07.090
And we'll see this a little
bit when we see another clip.

00:50:11.380 --> 00:50:14.590
But the fact that
people use rifles

00:50:14.590 --> 00:50:17.770
at this point, the rifles,
Flaherty deliberately

00:50:17.770 --> 00:50:22.280
had people not appear
with rifles in the film.

00:50:22.280 --> 00:50:24.320
He had them dress
in a way that people

00:50:24.320 --> 00:50:26.320
might have historically,
although in some cases,

00:50:26.320 --> 00:50:28.278
like, supposedly the
polar-skinned pants people

00:50:28.278 --> 00:50:29.620
in this area didn't wear.

00:50:29.620 --> 00:50:31.345
But he had them dress that way.

00:50:33.950 --> 00:50:36.550
So one of the ways that it
gets talked about by Flaherty

00:50:36.550 --> 00:50:38.140
was that he argues
at some point--

00:50:38.140 --> 00:50:39.643
what's the quote?

00:50:39.643 --> 00:50:40.810
I have it written down here.

00:50:40.810 --> 00:50:44.560
"That in order to capture
the essence of something,

00:50:44.560 --> 00:50:46.180
that one often has
to distort a thing

00:50:46.180 --> 00:50:48.610
to catch its true spirit."

00:50:48.610 --> 00:50:50.380
And what he did
a lot in the film

00:50:50.380 --> 00:50:55.030
was shape what he was
filming in a way that

00:50:55.030 --> 00:50:58.810
accorded with ideas of how he
thought Inuit people had been

00:50:58.810 --> 00:50:59.830
in the past.

00:50:59.830 --> 00:51:02.410
This kind of mythic
portrayal of them,

00:51:02.410 --> 00:51:06.400
what he assumed they would
have been like in a pure,

00:51:06.400 --> 00:51:09.790
quote, unquote, "past" before
contact with Europeans.

00:51:09.790 --> 00:51:11.620
And so he has this
kind of-- some people

00:51:11.620 --> 00:51:13.450
refer to as a
salvage ethnography

00:51:13.450 --> 00:51:15.640
kind of perspective where
it's kind of interest

00:51:15.640 --> 00:51:18.580
in these people, but also
interest in what they

00:51:18.580 --> 00:51:21.850
would have been like
prior to European contact.

00:51:21.850 --> 00:51:25.000
And so there's a lot of
taking certain things out

00:51:25.000 --> 00:51:28.720
of the picture that
didn't fit with that.

00:51:28.720 --> 00:51:31.300
The problematic
power relationships

00:51:31.300 --> 00:51:33.850
in which people were, the
economic relationships that

00:51:33.850 --> 00:51:34.960
were there, you don't
really see that.

00:51:34.960 --> 00:51:37.210
You don't see people
reflecting on their situation,

00:51:37.210 --> 00:51:39.168
although with subtitles,
granted, it would be--

00:51:39.168 --> 00:51:41.972
I mean, without subtitles that
would be more difficult to do.

00:51:41.972 --> 00:51:43.930
But you don't necessarily
get a sense of people

00:51:43.930 --> 00:51:46.090
as being complicated,
people who are

00:51:46.090 --> 00:51:50.485
having their whole range of
thoughts in relation to this.

00:51:50.485 --> 00:51:56.480
That scene with the gramophone
scene, so one thing--

00:51:56.480 --> 00:51:59.170
as Catarina knows from
the reading that she did--

00:51:59.170 --> 00:52:03.460
actually, Flaherty worked
very closely with Inuit

00:52:03.460 --> 00:52:05.247
people in the
making of the film.

00:52:05.247 --> 00:52:06.580
He was very dependent on people.

00:52:09.340 --> 00:52:11.320
The people that he was
working with day-to-day

00:52:11.320 --> 00:52:15.100
were his Inuit
friends and people

00:52:15.100 --> 00:52:19.960
that he was working with
as he made the film.

00:52:19.960 --> 00:52:22.990
And-- sorry, I just lost
my train of thought.

00:52:22.990 --> 00:52:26.260
So actually, the people that
he was working with actually

00:52:26.260 --> 00:52:28.390
had a fair amount--

00:52:28.390 --> 00:52:30.612
quite a bit of
technical knowledge

00:52:30.612 --> 00:52:32.320
that they gained by
working on this film.

00:52:32.320 --> 00:52:34.390
And so some of the
Inuit people that he

00:52:34.390 --> 00:52:36.993
worked with, they
took the camera apart,

00:52:36.993 --> 00:52:38.410
they put the camera
back together,

00:52:38.410 --> 00:52:40.718
they did some of the shooting.

00:52:40.718 --> 00:52:42.760
They were very involved
in the filmmaking process

00:52:42.760 --> 00:52:44.968
and in the technologies of
filmmaking in a way that's

00:52:44.968 --> 00:52:45.790
not acknowledged.

00:52:45.790 --> 00:52:47.940
And in that gramophone
scene-- what

00:52:47.940 --> 00:52:49.940
is the implication of
that gramophone scene when

00:52:49.940 --> 00:52:52.656
Nanook bites the record?

00:52:52.656 --> 00:52:54.390
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:54.390 --> 00:52:57.320
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah,
that is this portrayal

00:52:57.320 --> 00:53:01.020
that's that appears
to be showing, like,

00:53:01.020 --> 00:53:03.160
look how this is so
completely foreign to people,

00:53:03.160 --> 00:53:04.710
these kinds of
European technologies.

00:53:04.710 --> 00:53:06.000
Isn't it funny and
isn't it bizarre?

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:08.417
And this is so weird, and I
had to even take a bite of it.

00:53:08.417 --> 00:53:12.210
But the reality was that
Flaherty was working closely

00:53:12.210 --> 00:53:15.000
with the person who gets
called Nanook in the film.

00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:16.860
That wasn't his real name.

00:53:16.860 --> 00:53:20.430
And Nanook actually-- the
person who plays Nanook--

00:53:20.430 --> 00:53:22.470
suggested a lot of the
scenes, was very involved

00:53:22.470 --> 00:53:26.692
in thinking up the film,
again, and other people

00:53:26.692 --> 00:53:28.900
as well working a lot with
the technology to make it.

00:53:28.900 --> 00:53:31.170
But all of that gets
written out of the picture.

00:53:31.170 --> 00:53:32.130
You don't see that.

00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:33.450
That's outside the frame.

00:53:33.450 --> 00:53:35.200
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well,
there's a funny moment

00:53:35.200 --> 00:53:39.990
in the very first inner title
sequence, the very long one,

00:53:39.990 --> 00:53:42.780
where he says, poor
Nanook hung around,

00:53:42.780 --> 00:53:45.300
trying to get me to
stay for another year,

00:53:45.300 --> 00:53:48.150
talking about all the
great films we could make.

00:53:48.150 --> 00:53:51.780
And to me that kind of rings
true because filmmaking

00:53:51.780 --> 00:53:55.500
is really fun and this guy is
collaborating with Flaherty.

00:53:55.500 --> 00:53:57.880
And he doesn't want it to end.

00:53:57.880 --> 00:54:01.770
But the way, of course, it's
presented in the inner titles,

00:54:01.770 --> 00:54:08.460
it's this simple,
naive, but noble person

00:54:08.460 --> 00:54:11.160
who has this simplistic
idea of what they're doing.

00:54:11.160 --> 00:54:16.750
And isn't it charming that he
wants to continue to do it?

00:54:16.750 --> 00:54:19.110
But if you think of
him as a collaborator,

00:54:19.110 --> 00:54:21.060
it's like, wow, this is great.

00:54:21.060 --> 00:54:23.550
Let's keep doing this
because this is so exciting.

00:54:23.550 --> 00:54:29.460
And that one little thing,
really, to me, kind of rings

00:54:29.460 --> 00:54:30.187
very true.

00:54:30.187 --> 00:54:31.770
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
So as Inuit partners

00:54:31.770 --> 00:54:34.650
being these unacknowledged
collaborators in the film

00:54:34.650 --> 00:54:37.230
and, in part, being
unacknowledged because Flaherty

00:54:37.230 --> 00:54:41.970
wants to present this is
about a pre-European contact

00:54:41.970 --> 00:54:44.160
period where somehow
I'm capturing

00:54:44.160 --> 00:54:48.300
the essence of these
primitive people.

00:54:48.300 --> 00:54:51.210
And actually, what
people later discovered

00:54:51.210 --> 00:54:57.180
that wasn't actually an actual
family, that the women who get

00:54:57.180 --> 00:54:59.850
portrayed as the wives of Nanook
were actually the common law

00:54:59.850 --> 00:55:02.980
wives of Flaherty himself.

00:55:02.980 --> 00:55:05.490
It was not an uncommon thing
during the colonial period

00:55:05.490 --> 00:55:11.580
for European white
men in the colonies

00:55:11.580 --> 00:55:15.450
to have relationships
with women in the places

00:55:15.450 --> 00:55:16.200
they were working.

00:55:16.200 --> 00:55:18.630
He fathered children
in the north

00:55:18.630 --> 00:55:22.980
that were not acknowledged
by his American wife.

00:55:22.980 --> 00:55:25.590
So he had these very
complicated relationship

00:55:25.590 --> 00:55:27.600
to the Inuit people
that he was working

00:55:27.600 --> 00:55:30.990
with it that is completely
left out of the film.

00:55:30.990 --> 00:55:34.260
So on the one hand,
you have all of that.

00:55:34.260 --> 00:55:37.800
And on the other hand, one of
the issues that Catarina also

00:55:37.800 --> 00:55:42.570
raised is the kind
of reassuring sense

00:55:42.570 --> 00:55:45.780
that all these people appear
to be so different in the film,

00:55:45.780 --> 00:55:47.928
but they also seem very
familiar in a sense that,

00:55:47.928 --> 00:55:48.720
here's this family.

00:55:48.720 --> 00:55:52.730
Watch how the family is working.

00:55:52.730 --> 00:55:56.010
But something else to keep in
mind, I mean, this is something

00:55:56.010 --> 00:56:00.630
that was quite innovative
about what Flaherty was doing

00:56:00.630 --> 00:56:02.580
was the fact that he
was taking these images,

00:56:02.580 --> 00:56:05.580
but he was focusing on
particular individuals.

00:56:05.580 --> 00:56:07.950
And he was focusing on a family.

00:56:07.950 --> 00:56:10.110
A lot of photographs you
would find at the time

00:56:10.110 --> 00:56:14.850
during the colonial period,
and a lot of films after this

00:56:14.850 --> 00:56:17.850
as well would often focus
on non-western peoples

00:56:17.850 --> 00:56:19.950
as ethnic types.

00:56:19.950 --> 00:56:23.310
So you wouldn't necessarily
see photos, for example,

00:56:23.310 --> 00:56:24.065
of individuals.

00:56:24.065 --> 00:56:26.190
You might see a photograph
of an African woman that

00:56:26.190 --> 00:56:29.110
says Bantu woman with basket
on her head or something

00:56:29.110 --> 00:56:29.610
like that.

00:56:29.610 --> 00:56:32.850
Much of the visual imagery
was about these racial types

00:56:32.850 --> 00:56:34.230
rather than about individuals.

00:56:34.230 --> 00:56:37.350
And something that was
distinctive about this film

00:56:37.350 --> 00:56:41.800
was the focus on
particular individuals.

00:56:41.800 --> 00:56:46.973
So that was both breaking with
much of the contemporary usage

00:56:46.973 --> 00:56:48.390
at the time, and
it was also a way

00:56:48.390 --> 00:56:52.440
to pull audiences in because
audiences became fascinated

00:56:52.440 --> 00:56:55.170
with this family, with
particular individuals,

00:56:55.170 --> 00:56:57.700
and they end up following--
it becomes more of a story,

00:56:57.700 --> 00:56:59.850
it becomes more of a narrative.

00:56:59.850 --> 00:57:02.683
And that also, for
film theorists,

00:57:02.683 --> 00:57:05.100
seen as being one of the major
contributions of this film,

00:57:05.100 --> 00:57:10.140
that instead of random
images of different places,

00:57:10.140 --> 00:57:11.760
the pieces get put
together to create

00:57:11.760 --> 00:57:13.920
a story around this family.

00:57:13.920 --> 00:57:18.450
And the story being
about the struggle

00:57:18.450 --> 00:57:20.310
to survive in this
kind of context.

00:57:20.310 --> 00:57:21.810
CHRIS BOEBEL: We
should change tape.

00:57:21.810 --> 00:57:23.117
If you change tape.

00:57:23.117 --> 00:57:25.200
So we are going to take
just like a 2-minute break

00:57:25.200 --> 00:57:26.970
to change tape in the cameras.

00:57:32.072 --> 00:57:33.780
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So
just to recap a bit.

00:57:33.780 --> 00:57:37.490
So for Flaherty, then,
this idea that he

00:57:37.490 --> 00:57:41.060
had that many people at the time
had that the Eskimo way of life

00:57:41.060 --> 00:57:41.943
was disappearing.

00:57:41.943 --> 00:57:44.360
And so it was important to
capture the way that life would

00:57:44.360 --> 00:57:46.130
have been before
pre-European contact

00:57:46.130 --> 00:57:47.850
before that was completely lost.

00:57:47.850 --> 00:57:49.940
So that being part of
the idea of salvage

00:57:49.940 --> 00:57:53.120
ethnography at the time,
which even Flaherty was not

00:57:53.120 --> 00:57:55.220
an anthropologist,
but that being

00:57:55.220 --> 00:57:59.810
part of some of
the larger language

00:57:59.810 --> 00:58:05.300
around the stuff that he would
have been participating in.

00:58:05.300 --> 00:58:07.790
But let's, before
we go on, let's just

00:58:07.790 --> 00:58:10.700
talk a little bit about
how this was shot.

00:58:10.700 --> 00:58:12.950
So how was it shot?

00:58:12.950 --> 00:58:14.430
CHRIS BOEBEL: Any ideas?

00:58:14.430 --> 00:58:15.810
Thoughts?

00:58:15.810 --> 00:58:17.810
CHRISTINE WALLEY: About,
how is it put together?

00:58:17.810 --> 00:58:20.390
What was the impact of the
way that it was put together?

00:58:20.390 --> 00:58:22.910
CHRIS BOEBEL: I mean, one way
to think about it, for those

00:58:22.910 --> 00:58:28.490
of you who were here last week,
think about the way the Lumiere

00:58:28.490 --> 00:58:32.420
shorts were shot, some of
the other stuff we've seen,

00:58:32.420 --> 00:58:34.870
and compare to this.

00:58:34.870 --> 00:58:37.440
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
because otherwise I'm

00:58:37.440 --> 00:58:39.860
just going to be [INAUDIBLE].

00:58:39.860 --> 00:58:42.590
And then I also heard somehow
that they showed it to Nanook,

00:58:42.590 --> 00:58:44.090
or, like, I don't
know if it said it

00:58:44.090 --> 00:58:46.002
on the screen [INAUDIBLE].

00:58:46.002 --> 00:58:46.960
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

00:58:46.960 --> 00:58:49.300
So again, in terms of his
collaborative relationships

00:58:49.300 --> 00:58:51.092
with Inuit people that
he was working with,

00:58:51.092 --> 00:58:53.470
they would have been
seeing the rushes

00:58:53.470 --> 00:58:58.000
as they were created and also
had input into what was shot.

00:58:58.000 --> 00:59:01.102
So supposedly Nanook and some
of the other people had ideas.

00:59:01.102 --> 00:59:02.560
And as Chris was
mentioned earlier,

00:59:02.560 --> 00:59:05.530
that the person who plays
the person of Nanook

00:59:05.530 --> 00:59:07.390
was really into the
whole filmmaking process

00:59:07.390 --> 00:59:08.830
and supposedly was always
coming up with ideas--

00:59:08.830 --> 00:59:10.372
why don't we shoot
this, why don't we

00:59:10.372 --> 00:59:12.880
shoot this-- because he was
having fun and enjoying it.

00:59:12.880 --> 00:59:16.390
But so Flaherty was also
getting feedback from people

00:59:16.390 --> 00:59:17.440
as he was shooting it.

00:59:17.440 --> 00:59:20.320
And some people have argued that
even the aesthetic sensibility

00:59:20.320 --> 00:59:23.980
of the film, in the clips we saw
you didn't see so much of it.

00:59:23.980 --> 00:59:26.170
But some of them are
just these gorgeous pans

00:59:26.170 --> 00:59:30.670
across the landscape that
just have the snow and ice,

00:59:30.670 --> 00:59:33.250
and then you might see a
few dogs in the distance.

00:59:33.250 --> 00:59:37.210
And it's very similar to
Inuit painting styles.

00:59:37.210 --> 00:59:40.510
And some people have argued
either that Flaherty was deeply

00:59:40.510 --> 00:59:42.293
influenced by Inuit
painting styles,

00:59:42.293 --> 00:59:44.710
or it might have been also
some of his Inuit collaborators

00:59:44.710 --> 00:59:46.990
themselves using the camera
and shooting it according

00:59:46.990 --> 00:59:48.070
to their own aesthetics.

00:59:48.070 --> 00:59:52.180
So again, this kind
of Inuit influence

00:59:52.180 --> 00:59:56.020
on what Flaherty was doing,
that's not really more formally

00:59:56.020 --> 00:59:58.000
acknowledged.

00:59:58.000 --> 01:00:01.540
But other thoughts on
how it was put together?

01:00:01.540 --> 01:00:02.290
[? Anita? ?]

01:00:02.290 --> 01:00:03.873
AUDIENCE: It was
interesting because I

01:00:03.873 --> 01:00:06.520
think it's very similar to the
kind of movies we watch today.

01:00:06.520 --> 01:00:08.500
And there's not an
establishing shot,

01:00:08.500 --> 01:00:12.900
like going with close ups.
And it's very interesting

01:00:12.900 --> 01:00:15.270
how it exactly
follows that, or I

01:00:15.270 --> 01:00:19.350
guess that's what primarily
invented the kind of idea

01:00:19.350 --> 01:00:22.890
of using different shots
to tell the story instead

01:00:22.890 --> 01:00:27.520
of like personalize the story
and show these characters

01:00:27.520 --> 01:00:29.928
as an individual.

01:00:29.928 --> 01:00:31.970
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, one
thing that is interesting

01:00:31.970 --> 01:00:35.030
is that in the 20 years
between Cheese Mites

01:00:35.030 --> 01:00:36.530
and some of the
other shorts that we

01:00:36.530 --> 01:00:38.360
saw last week and this--

01:00:38.360 --> 01:00:39.950
or slightly more than 20 years--

01:00:39.950 --> 01:00:42.560
film grammar has been
really established.

01:00:42.560 --> 01:00:47.450
DW Griffith has been pioneering
that with fiction films,

01:00:47.450 --> 01:00:51.452
narrative films, which
again, have a lot of overlap

01:00:51.452 --> 01:00:52.910
with "documentary,"
quote, unquote,

01:00:52.910 --> 01:00:54.200
which isn't even really
a term that people

01:00:54.200 --> 01:00:55.400
are using at this point.

01:00:55.400 --> 01:00:58.220
But yeah, close ups,
establishing shots,

01:00:58.220 --> 01:01:03.650
pans, all of those are really
new and kind of exciting things

01:01:03.650 --> 01:01:06.950
that by this point, people are
used to watching in theaters.

01:01:06.950 --> 01:01:08.510
But they're not that old.

01:01:08.510 --> 01:01:12.920
And it completely
changes the way

01:01:12.920 --> 01:01:15.930
the camera and then the audience
"receives the information,"

01:01:15.930 --> 01:01:21.110
quote, unquote, and the
way that the stories are

01:01:21.110 --> 01:01:24.320
edited, created, manipulated.

01:01:24.320 --> 01:01:26.480
And I'm not using that
term in a pejorative way,

01:01:26.480 --> 01:01:28.730
although obviously there's
a lot of manipulation here.

01:01:31.370 --> 01:01:33.470
And it gives you just
all of this latitude.

01:01:33.470 --> 01:01:38.120
I mean, think about how the
characters are introduced,

01:01:38.120 --> 01:01:42.410
Nanook and his family.

01:01:42.410 --> 01:01:45.260
Title, name, close up.

01:01:45.260 --> 01:01:48.170
And the close up just
extends for a long time.

01:01:48.170 --> 01:01:50.270
And that's basically
telling you,

01:01:50.270 --> 01:01:51.920
pay attention to these people.

01:01:51.920 --> 01:01:55.250
Pay attention to
these human beings.

01:01:55.250 --> 01:01:58.698
And you're just asked
to, right at eye level--

01:01:58.698 --> 01:02:00.740
camera's right at eye
level-- look at their faces

01:02:00.740 --> 01:02:04.590
for a very long time or a
comparatively long time.

01:02:04.590 --> 01:02:09.290
And that is something that
just completely locates you

01:02:09.290 --> 01:02:12.320
in what the film is
going to be about,

01:02:12.320 --> 01:02:15.500
who it's going to be about
in a very powerful way, which

01:02:15.500 --> 01:02:17.630
of course, to us, is
kind of second nature

01:02:17.630 --> 01:02:19.190
because we read
films all the time.

01:02:19.190 --> 01:02:22.470
But it's a big deal.

01:02:22.470 --> 01:02:24.542
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Also,
just some of these terms,

01:02:24.542 --> 01:02:26.750
in case some of you aren't
aware, establishing shots.

01:02:26.750 --> 01:02:27.792
Do you know what that is?

01:02:33.510 --> 01:02:36.200
A wide shot of whatever
the place is, so

01:02:36.200 --> 01:02:38.700
that when a viewer sees it they
know, ah, this is the place.

01:02:38.700 --> 01:02:39.540
So for example, if
you're going to do

01:02:39.540 --> 01:02:42.450
an establishing shot of MIT,
you might stand on Mass Ave.

01:02:42.450 --> 01:02:43.290
and do the big dome.

01:02:43.290 --> 01:02:47.735
Sort of like, ah, what you're
going to see next is at MIT.

01:02:47.735 --> 01:02:49.110
So here, establishing
shots helps

01:02:49.110 --> 01:02:51.540
the viewer locate where
the action is going to be.

01:02:51.540 --> 01:02:56.970
CHRIS BOEBEL: Or in the Eskimo
or the igloo building scene,

01:02:56.970 --> 01:02:59.320
a wide shot that shows
the people working,

01:02:59.320 --> 01:03:00.870
and the kids over
here, and where

01:03:00.870 --> 01:03:02.310
the igloo is taking shape.

01:03:02.310 --> 01:03:04.680
So you're kind of
located in space.

01:03:04.680 --> 01:03:07.710
And then you might go into
close ups of the knives,

01:03:07.710 --> 01:03:09.960
and the people working,
and the kids playing.

01:03:09.960 --> 01:03:12.990
The scene gets fragmented
after the establishing shot.

01:03:12.990 --> 01:03:16.080
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And also,
reaction shots, which Flaherty

01:03:16.080 --> 01:03:18.750
didn't have the earlier version
of the film that burned,

01:03:18.750 --> 01:03:20.500
but he has in this version.

01:03:20.500 --> 01:03:23.700
So again, coming out
of the film grammar

01:03:23.700 --> 01:03:26.460
that's emerging out of fiction
film, so reaction shots

01:03:26.460 --> 01:03:29.670
then being where you see action
happening with one person

01:03:29.670 --> 01:03:31.570
and then another person
reacting to that.

01:03:31.570 --> 01:03:33.237
And in order to do
that with the camera,

01:03:33.237 --> 01:03:35.070
you really have to
have two cameras going.

01:03:35.070 --> 01:03:37.470
Or you have to reshoot
something and reenact something

01:03:37.470 --> 01:03:39.887
with a different camera angle
if you only have one camera.

01:03:42.333 --> 01:03:44.250
CHRIS BOEBEL: So I mean,
imagine in this class

01:03:44.250 --> 01:03:47.430
if we had one camera
and not three,

01:03:47.430 --> 01:03:50.070
and we ask you all to
stay after and reproduce

01:03:50.070 --> 01:03:52.410
all your comments.

01:03:52.410 --> 01:03:54.510
That would be one
camera shooting

01:03:54.510 --> 01:03:56.370
as if we had multiple cameras.

01:03:56.370 --> 01:04:00.390
And that's essentially
what Flaherty was doing.

01:04:00.390 --> 01:04:03.240
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And
there's a very nice quote

01:04:03.240 --> 01:04:05.760
in Barnouw, one of the
readings for today,

01:04:05.760 --> 01:04:08.310
that talks about this issue.

01:04:08.310 --> 01:04:09.840
"Flaherty had
apparently mastered,

01:04:09.840 --> 01:04:13.050
unlike previous
documentarists,"--

01:04:13.050 --> 01:04:15.000
excuse me-- "the
grammar of film as it

01:04:15.000 --> 01:04:17.068
had evolved in the film
and the fiction film.

01:04:17.068 --> 01:04:19.110
This evolution had not
merely changed techniques,

01:04:19.110 --> 01:04:21.720
it had transformed the
sensibilities of audiences.

01:04:21.720 --> 01:04:23.310
The ability to
witness an episode

01:04:23.310 --> 01:04:26.760
for many angles and distances
seen in quick succession,

01:04:26.760 --> 01:04:30.150
a totally surrealistic privilege
unmatched in human experience."

01:04:30.150 --> 01:04:32.970
I mean, again, if you
just had a camera, that's

01:04:32.970 --> 01:04:36.240
not the way the camera would
actually be seeing or recording

01:04:36.240 --> 01:04:36.900
things.

01:04:36.900 --> 01:04:38.400
"Had become so
much a part of film

01:04:38.400 --> 01:04:40.080
viewing that it
was unconsciously

01:04:40.080 --> 01:04:42.180
accepted as natural."

01:04:42.180 --> 01:04:45.143
So much of the shooting style
that we just take for granted,

01:04:45.143 --> 01:04:47.310
this is showing us what's
really going on the place,

01:04:47.310 --> 01:04:49.155
is an artificial construction.

01:04:49.155 --> 01:04:51.030
But we don't necessarily
recognize it as such

01:04:51.030 --> 01:04:53.490
because we're so used
to it being put together

01:04:53.490 --> 01:04:54.400
in that kind of way.

01:04:54.400 --> 01:04:55.110
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
it relates back

01:04:55.110 --> 01:04:56.527
to the whole
question of the frame

01:04:56.527 --> 01:04:58.410
that we talked about
in the first class.

01:04:58.410 --> 01:05:02.040
And it's going to be just a
huge preoccupation for everyone

01:05:02.040 --> 01:05:03.960
as you make your own films.

01:05:03.960 --> 01:05:05.370
What do you put in the frame?

01:05:05.370 --> 01:05:07.255
And not just in a
particular shot,

01:05:07.255 --> 01:05:08.880
but how does what
you put in that frame

01:05:08.880 --> 01:05:11.100
relate to what you put in
the frame of the next shot,

01:05:11.100 --> 01:05:12.360
and the shot after that?

01:05:12.360 --> 01:05:14.102
And how does one
influence the other?

01:05:14.102 --> 01:05:15.810
And then, when you're
editing, what order

01:05:15.810 --> 01:05:16.740
do you put it all in?

01:05:16.740 --> 01:05:17.850
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Mm-hmm.

01:05:17.850 --> 01:05:19.500
And one other issue
to think about here

01:05:19.500 --> 01:05:21.508
is, how is the editing done?

01:05:21.508 --> 01:05:23.550
So the editing here being
described as continuity

01:05:23.550 --> 01:05:24.630
editing.

01:05:24.630 --> 01:05:27.570
In other words, the editing
is done in such a way

01:05:27.570 --> 01:05:29.940
as to give the viewer
the sense of illusion,

01:05:29.940 --> 01:05:32.280
a realist sense of illusion
of being in the place

01:05:32.280 --> 01:05:36.242
and experiencing things,
which is, as you'll see later

01:05:36.242 --> 01:05:38.700
when we see the Vertov film
with man with the movie camera,

01:05:38.700 --> 01:05:40.783
completely different from
the kind of editing that

01:05:40.783 --> 01:05:43.230
was coming out of
Soviet filmmaking

01:05:43.230 --> 01:05:44.920
during this time period.

01:05:44.920 --> 01:05:46.770
So a certain kind
of editing meant

01:05:46.770 --> 01:05:50.730
to capture that sense
that you're in a place, so

01:05:50.730 --> 01:05:52.710
a realistic sensibility.

01:05:52.710 --> 01:05:54.900
But it's a realistic
sensibility, but, again,

01:05:54.900 --> 01:05:57.690
keep in mind that's not
how the eye or the camera

01:05:57.690 --> 01:06:00.743
necessarily actually sees.

01:06:00.743 --> 01:06:02.910
That's a construction that
we take as natural rather

01:06:02.910 --> 01:06:04.858
than it simply being that.

01:06:04.858 --> 01:06:06.900
CHRIS BOEBEL: And we're
so used to watching films

01:06:06.900 --> 01:06:09.840
at this point that
we don't even--

01:06:09.840 --> 01:06:11.525
I mean, unless we
suddenly become

01:06:11.525 --> 01:06:13.650
interested in how films
are constructed, most of us

01:06:13.650 --> 01:06:14.800
don't even think about it.

01:06:14.800 --> 01:06:17.730
We don't even think about the
multiple shots, and angles,

01:06:17.730 --> 01:06:20.550
and how they're
fitting together,

01:06:20.550 --> 01:06:23.130
and how our brain and our
experience of watching

01:06:23.130 --> 01:06:26.815
other films is assisting us
in making sense of something.

01:06:26.815 --> 01:06:27.690
CHRISTINE WALLEY: OK.

01:06:27.690 --> 01:06:29.550
So these kinds of
questions about how

01:06:29.550 --> 01:06:31.470
the film is put
together, we're going

01:06:31.470 --> 01:06:35.190
to be talking about more when we
see Vertov's Man with the Movie

01:06:35.190 --> 01:06:36.990
Camera that was made in 1929.

01:06:36.990 --> 01:06:39.540
But before we jump to
that, just to follow up

01:06:39.540 --> 01:06:41.880
on this question of
representations of Inuit

01:06:41.880 --> 01:06:46.380
a little bit, I just want
to show you a short bit

01:06:46.380 --> 01:06:49.050
from a film that was
made in the '90s called

01:06:49.050 --> 01:06:55.110
Nanook Revisited that was done
by a French filmmaker who went

01:06:55.110 --> 01:06:59.610
back into the area where Nanook
was shot and talked to people,

01:06:59.610 --> 01:07:02.940
Inuit people who lived in that
area about the making of Nanook

01:07:02.940 --> 01:07:05.801
of the North and
their reactions to it.

01:07:05.801 --> 01:07:06.468
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:07:06.468 --> 01:07:09.402
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:07:17.226 --> 01:07:19.200
We need a little light on.

01:07:19.200 --> 01:07:20.400
Can we get a little light?

01:07:20.400 --> 01:07:22.730
We just need to be
able to fast forward.

01:07:22.730 --> 01:07:24.006
Oh, never mind.

01:07:40.800 --> 01:07:46.040
- Friday, April 18, 1988, we
are now flying towards Inukjuak,

01:07:46.040 --> 01:07:49.070
at the north eastern
borders of the Hudson Bay,

01:07:49.070 --> 01:07:50.900
hardly ever noted on maps.

01:07:50.900 --> 01:07:52.610
Nevertheless, this
small village has

01:07:52.610 --> 01:07:55.550
been the seat of a
cinematographic adventure which

01:07:55.550 --> 01:07:58.880
will create decisive
change in cinema

01:07:58.880 --> 01:08:01.850
after doing so with the life
of the little Eskimo community.

01:08:12.570 --> 01:08:15.420
The scene takes place in 1920.

01:08:15.420 --> 01:08:18.210
An American of Irish
origins decides

01:08:18.210 --> 01:08:22.109
to go and shoot a film on the
life of a small Eskimo group.

01:08:22.109 --> 01:08:24.450
Nowadays, we call
it a documentary.

01:08:24.450 --> 01:08:26.430
But at the time, it
didn't have a name

01:08:26.430 --> 01:08:29.970
and the plan seemed extravagant.

01:08:29.970 --> 01:08:32.189
The film is called Nanook.

01:08:32.189 --> 01:08:34.620
The filmmaker, Robert Flaherty.

01:08:34.620 --> 01:08:38.100
For the historians of cinema,
he is the father of documentary.

01:09:11.310 --> 01:09:15.479
Today, we are heading towards
Inukjuak, 68 years after him,

01:09:15.479 --> 01:09:17.609
bringing with us
a tape of his film

01:09:17.609 --> 01:09:21.188
and 70 pictures found in
the archives of [INAUDIBLE]..

01:09:27.050 --> 01:09:31.580
Flaherty enters Inukjuak,
then called Port Harrison,

01:09:31.580 --> 01:09:33.950
during summer, 1920.

01:09:33.950 --> 01:09:37.550
He will stay there a year.

01:09:37.550 --> 01:09:39.500
He writes in his journal.

01:09:39.500 --> 01:09:42.710
"On August 15, we drop anchor
at the mouth of the Innuksuak

01:09:42.710 --> 01:09:43.430
River.

01:09:43.430 --> 01:09:45.649
Less than half a mile
away, we discover

01:09:45.649 --> 01:09:49.880
fastened onto the hillside, five
desolated and melancholy huts

01:09:49.880 --> 01:09:52.069
which formed the
whole of the station.

01:09:52.069 --> 01:09:54.590
The inhabitants of Port
Harrison are the poorest people

01:09:54.590 --> 01:09:55.520
I've ever seen.

01:09:59.090 --> 01:10:01.490
The day following our
arrival, the mayor

01:10:01.490 --> 01:10:03.830
announces on the radio
that we are organizing

01:10:03.830 --> 01:10:05.060
a projection of Nanook."

01:10:05.060 --> 01:10:07.740
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

01:10:09.350 --> 01:10:15.410
This film of Robert Flaherty
was shot towards 1920,

01:10:15.410 --> 01:10:16.400
a long time ago.

01:10:20.270 --> 01:10:22.070
Today, you're
shooting a film on us,

01:10:22.070 --> 01:10:25.790
but Flaherty's was
the very first one.

01:10:30.470 --> 01:10:32.810
This great film has been
seen all over the world.

01:10:36.860 --> 01:10:39.320
Our friends, filmmakers
come from the other side

01:10:39.320 --> 01:10:42.665
of the ocean from a far country.

01:10:50.110 --> 01:10:52.300
They're going to watch
with them this film

01:10:52.300 --> 01:10:54.529
and pictures of those times.

01:10:54.529 --> 01:10:57.463
[END PLAYBACK]

01:11:01.512 --> 01:11:03.470
CHRISTINE WALLEY: OK, so
you saw a bit of that.

01:11:03.470 --> 01:11:04.803
And again, I'll leave the rest--

01:11:04.803 --> 01:11:08.920
I'll leave the VHS tapes in DV
lab in case anybody wants to,

01:11:08.920 --> 01:11:13.930
you can make appointments with
Amberly Steward in the anthro

01:11:13.930 --> 01:11:15.747
office if you want to
borrow our TV and VCR

01:11:15.747 --> 01:11:17.830
and watch it in the small
conference room we have,

01:11:17.830 --> 01:11:21.220
in case anybody wants to
see the rest of these films.

01:11:21.220 --> 01:11:23.080
But I also just
wanted to show you

01:11:23.080 --> 01:11:25.180
a little bit of a
piece called Starting

01:11:25.180 --> 01:11:28.930
Fire with Gunpowder that
was made by Inuit media

01:11:28.930 --> 01:11:30.157
makers themselves.

01:11:30.157 --> 01:11:31.240
I mean one of the things--

01:11:31.240 --> 01:11:32.620
I mean, so you have
Nanook Revisited,

01:11:32.620 --> 01:11:34.180
which is an interesting
historical perspective

01:11:34.180 --> 01:11:34.840
in the '80s.

01:11:34.840 --> 01:11:39.790
At that point, as you can
see from the documentary,

01:11:39.790 --> 01:11:43.180
media making by
Inuit producers was

01:11:43.180 --> 01:11:47.540
becoming a quite interesting
movement in this region,

01:11:47.540 --> 01:11:50.170
both in fiction film and in
terms of documentary film.

01:11:50.170 --> 01:11:53.170
I don't know-- how many have
heard of the film Antanarjuat--

01:11:53.170 --> 01:11:55.150
I'm not sure exactly
how to pronounce it--

01:11:55.150 --> 01:11:57.450
The Fast Runner?

01:11:57.450 --> 01:11:59.350
I think it was
about five years ago

01:11:59.350 --> 01:12:01.840
it won a major award at Cannes.

01:12:01.840 --> 01:12:06.580
It was the first
Inuit-written, acted,

01:12:06.580 --> 01:12:08.960
produced fiction film
that did quite well.

01:12:08.960 --> 01:12:10.990
And we're actually going
to see Zach Kunuk has

01:12:10.990 --> 01:12:13.120
another documentary film
that he just recently made

01:12:13.120 --> 01:12:15.287
about climate change that
we're going to be watching

01:12:15.287 --> 01:12:16.820
a little later in class.

01:12:16.820 --> 01:12:18.400
But also in this
region, you have,

01:12:18.400 --> 01:12:20.320
through the Canadian
government, you

01:12:20.320 --> 01:12:24.760
had Inuit peoples who were
living in this area were

01:12:24.760 --> 01:12:27.970
able to obtain funds.

01:12:27.970 --> 01:12:31.850
They pressed for funds to
fund some of their own TV film

01:12:31.850 --> 01:12:32.350
program.

01:12:32.350 --> 01:12:34.630
So there's also
documentary work that's

01:12:34.630 --> 01:12:37.420
been made by Inuit
documentary makers

01:12:37.420 --> 01:12:39.190
for local TV audiences
in this area.

01:12:39.190 --> 01:12:41.690
So I'm just going to let you
see a couple of minutes of that

01:12:41.690 --> 01:12:45.963
so you get a sense of what some
of that image making is like.

01:12:45.963 --> 01:12:46.630
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:12:46.630 --> 01:12:48.730
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

01:12:48.730 --> 01:12:51.730
[NON-ENGLISH CHANTING]

01:13:09.570 --> 01:13:11.700
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

01:13:38.220 --> 01:13:42.450
- I think Inuit are not
shy of television or radio.

01:13:42.450 --> 01:13:46.920
One of the main reasons
is that they've always

01:13:46.920 --> 01:13:52.110
been very communicating
kind of people.

01:13:52.110 --> 01:13:54.960
But it has been very
slow because they

01:13:54.960 --> 01:13:59.290
would take messages from
one community to another,

01:13:59.290 --> 01:14:02.370
from one region to
another only by adopting.

01:14:02.370 --> 01:14:04.800
It was only through
word of mouth

01:14:04.800 --> 01:14:07.770
that they were able to
pass this message on.

01:14:10.650 --> 01:14:15.090
Not having any written
history, this form

01:14:15.090 --> 01:14:17.370
of communication,
radio and television,

01:14:17.370 --> 01:14:25.710
was as old as it was new, an
oral form of communication.

01:14:25.710 --> 01:14:27.490
- [INAUDIBLE] is great.

01:14:27.490 --> 01:14:30.120
We are a communicator people.

01:14:30.120 --> 01:14:32.910
Our language is
called Inuktitut.

01:14:32.910 --> 01:14:35.370
They are far more films
and television shows

01:14:35.370 --> 01:14:40.110
made in Inuktitut than there are
books written in our language.

01:14:40.110 --> 01:14:42.240
I am Ann Meekitjuk Hanson.

01:14:42.240 --> 01:14:45.810
I'm a broadcaster, a
journalist, and a storyteller.

01:14:45.810 --> 01:14:48.938
This is a story of
Inuit television.

01:14:48.938 --> 01:14:52.354
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:14:55.780 --> 01:15:01.050
[INAUDIBLE] White man's TV
arrives suddenly in the Arctic.

01:15:01.050 --> 01:15:04.080
It was very seductive
to many of us.

01:15:04.080 --> 01:15:07.530
But the exposure to that
much English language

01:15:07.530 --> 01:15:12.000
could have wiped out our
culture in a single generation.

01:15:12.000 --> 01:15:17.370
We had to find our own
broadcasting voice.

01:15:17.370 --> 01:15:21.270
Inuit television started as
a backlash in Northern Quebec

01:15:21.270 --> 01:15:23.550
where the introduction
of white man's TV

01:15:23.550 --> 01:15:26.130
was met by stiff resistance.

01:15:26.130 --> 01:15:28.050
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

01:16:07.735 --> 01:16:10.597
[END PLAYBACK]

01:16:12.807 --> 01:16:14.390
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
OK, unfortunately we

01:16:14.390 --> 01:16:18.300
don't have time to watch more,
but it's really interesting.

01:16:18.300 --> 01:16:19.890
So this soap opera
partly dealing

01:16:19.890 --> 01:16:26.970
with issues of substance abuse
in the area among young people,

01:16:26.970 --> 01:16:35.150
and it took the part of
things like glue sniffing.

01:16:35.150 --> 01:16:37.550
Anyway, so there's probably
educational soap operas,

01:16:37.550 --> 01:16:39.368
and there's a really
interesting series

01:16:39.368 --> 01:16:40.910
that they did that's
kind of an Inuit

01:16:40.910 --> 01:16:43.730
version of Superman for
kids, which is really fun,

01:16:43.730 --> 01:16:45.980
[INAUDIBLE].

01:16:45.980 --> 01:16:48.960
But anyway, so if anybody wants
to borrow it, I recommend it.

01:16:48.960 --> 01:16:52.010
It's a really
interesting documentary

01:16:52.010 --> 01:16:54.800
about Inuit media making.

01:16:54.800 --> 01:16:59.210
So reactions, thoughts
about all this?

01:17:04.980 --> 01:17:08.145
I mean, the kinds of
representations, well,

01:17:08.145 --> 01:17:10.020
different questions for
each, but let's maybe

01:17:10.020 --> 01:17:11.437
talk about Nanook
Revisited first,

01:17:11.437 --> 01:17:13.080
which was the first
one that you saw.

01:17:13.080 --> 01:17:19.350
So at least in the bit
that you were able to see,

01:17:19.350 --> 01:17:26.490
how was Nanook understood
at that historical point

01:17:26.490 --> 01:17:29.700
when they were filming
in the '90s, late '80s?

01:17:32.742 --> 01:17:34.200
AUDIENCE: It seemed
like they still

01:17:34.200 --> 01:17:36.120
had their relatives, like,
their grandparents might

01:17:36.120 --> 01:17:37.110
have been in the film.

01:17:37.110 --> 01:17:38.860
And then it's also
interesting to see how,

01:17:38.860 --> 01:17:40.428
since it was filmed
in the late '80s,

01:17:40.428 --> 01:17:41.970
and this is like,
oh, the modern view

01:17:41.970 --> 01:17:44.340
of what it's like now there,
and it's kind of like that.

01:17:44.340 --> 01:17:47.805
It was like 20 or 30 years ago.

01:17:47.805 --> 01:17:50.910
[INAUDIBLE] Takes you
back in time again.

01:17:53.420 --> 01:17:56.500
AUDIENCE: It will be interesting
to see why people were, like,

01:17:56.500 --> 01:17:58.570
what was funny about the film.

01:17:58.570 --> 01:18:00.610
They were laughing so loud.

01:18:00.610 --> 01:18:02.650
Was it because--

01:18:02.650 --> 01:18:04.660
I mean, the film
already was representing

01:18:04.660 --> 01:18:07.550
things that would have
been funny at the time.

01:18:07.550 --> 01:18:10.150
So probably even funnier
for people who remembered

01:18:10.150 --> 01:18:12.000
who were children then.

01:18:12.000 --> 01:18:14.942
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it's home
movies in some ways, yeah.

01:18:14.942 --> 01:18:16.557
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Uh-huh, Ben?

01:18:16.557 --> 01:18:17.890
AUDIENCE: I find it interesting.

01:18:17.890 --> 01:18:20.530
It's kind of like deja vu,
for lack of better words

01:18:20.530 --> 01:18:24.370
and how they all packed the
hall to watch the movie,

01:18:24.370 --> 01:18:28.160
that everyone packed to watch
six to eight years before.

01:18:28.160 --> 01:18:32.982
Now it's still, in a sense, it
brought the community together.

01:18:32.982 --> 01:18:33.940
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

01:18:33.940 --> 01:18:34.440
Yeah.

01:18:34.440 --> 01:18:37.210
I mean, so you have kind of
two different things going on.

01:18:37.210 --> 01:18:39.910
On the one hand,
as people say, this

01:18:39.910 --> 01:18:41.980
is an important
historical record.

01:18:41.980 --> 01:18:47.440
Because those kinds of visual
documents from the time period

01:18:47.440 --> 01:18:48.980
aren't available otherwise.

01:18:48.980 --> 01:18:50.800
And so, as it gets
mentioned in the film,

01:18:50.800 --> 01:18:54.160
in some ways the images and the
photographs taken by Flaherty

01:18:54.160 --> 01:18:59.290
become this big family album
where people get to, like, ah,

01:18:59.290 --> 01:19:01.120
I remember so-and-so,
I remember so-and-so.

01:19:01.120 --> 01:19:02.290
And all this
laughing, yeah, it's

01:19:02.290 --> 01:19:04.390
hard to know exactly what
the laughing is about.

01:19:04.390 --> 01:19:07.300
I mean, is it just the fun of
seeing themselves in people

01:19:07.300 --> 01:19:09.940
they know, or is it also because
the construction of the film

01:19:09.940 --> 01:19:11.960
is different than the way that
they would have put it together

01:19:11.960 --> 01:19:13.010
and so it seems
funny in that way?

01:19:13.010 --> 01:19:14.427
I mean, it might
be a bit of both.

01:19:15.862 --> 01:19:17.320
CHRIS BOEBEL: People
that they know

01:19:17.320 --> 01:19:18.790
dressed in polar bear pants.

01:19:18.790 --> 01:19:19.790
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Right.

01:19:19.790 --> 01:19:20.330
Yes.

01:19:20.330 --> 01:19:20.830
Exactly.

01:19:20.830 --> 01:19:22.747
Which, again, was done
for Southern audiences.

01:19:22.747 --> 01:19:28.120
So it might be some
mix of the two.

01:19:28.120 --> 01:19:30.610
So you have-- I just
lost my train of thought.

01:19:34.750 --> 01:19:36.560
I had some point
that I was leading to

01:19:36.560 --> 01:19:38.900
and I've just forgotten it,
but it'll come back to me.

01:19:38.900 --> 01:19:40.900
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well, one
quick point I would make

01:19:40.900 --> 01:19:44.140
is it's also interesting to,
then, start to deconstruct

01:19:44.140 --> 01:19:46.490
Nanook Revisited.

01:19:46.490 --> 01:19:48.220
You have this, what
we were talking

01:19:48.220 --> 01:19:52.180
about as the voice of God
narration in the first class.

01:19:52.180 --> 01:19:55.390
Very tried and true documentary
technique that leads

01:19:55.390 --> 01:19:57.550
to a certain kind of
relationship or feeling

01:19:57.550 --> 01:20:00.670
with the subject.

01:20:00.670 --> 01:20:02.470
This guy with a
plummy British accent

01:20:02.470 --> 01:20:08.230
translating what the Inuit women
are saying, and pronouncing

01:20:08.230 --> 01:20:10.570
the situation that you're
going to be finding yourself

01:20:10.570 --> 01:20:12.400
in the film.

01:20:12.400 --> 01:20:16.180
So it's interesting because
the point of the film--

01:20:16.180 --> 01:20:18.580
and it does it quite
effectively in many places--

01:20:18.580 --> 01:20:23.050
is to kind of deconstruct Nanook
of the North and deconstruct

01:20:23.050 --> 01:20:24.700
Flaherty's technique.

01:20:24.700 --> 01:20:29.530
But at the same time, it's also
constructing its own reality

01:20:29.530 --> 01:20:32.320
and its own point of
view with this narration

01:20:32.320 --> 01:20:36.130
and with the people flying
in at the beginning,

01:20:36.130 --> 01:20:37.550
swooping into the village.

01:20:37.550 --> 01:20:41.440
I mean, it's a very
interesting thing

01:20:41.440 --> 01:20:44.680
where they're deconstructing
this other film

01:20:44.680 --> 01:20:45.910
for very legitimate reasons.

01:20:45.910 --> 01:20:48.010
But that other film is
also extremely personal

01:20:48.010 --> 01:20:52.450
and extremely oriented
towards people in a way

01:20:52.450 --> 01:20:54.850
that that one isn't quite.

01:20:54.850 --> 01:20:58.033
So quite a dissonance there.

01:20:58.033 --> 01:21:00.200
CHRISTINE WALLEY: The point
I was thinking was that,

01:21:00.200 --> 01:21:03.893
so on the one hand, you have
people responding to Nanook,

01:21:03.893 --> 01:21:06.310
again, as if it's a family
album and it's really important

01:21:06.310 --> 01:21:07.100
to have these images.

01:21:07.100 --> 01:21:09.267
On the other hand, you also
hear critical commentary

01:21:09.267 --> 01:21:11.050
like one of the TV
producers talking

01:21:11.050 --> 01:21:14.500
about the gramophone scene, I
mean, clearly not being happy

01:21:14.500 --> 01:21:15.950
about the portrayal there.

01:21:15.950 --> 01:21:19.132
And so people were
being somewhat critical

01:21:19.132 --> 01:21:21.340
of the representations, but
at the same time thinking

01:21:21.340 --> 01:21:22.960
that it's really, again,
a really important

01:21:22.960 --> 01:21:24.650
historical record
for people to have,

01:21:24.650 --> 01:21:27.070
so this kind of complicated
relationship to the film.

01:21:27.070 --> 01:21:29.350
And then we have Starting
Fire with Gunpowder, which

01:21:29.350 --> 01:21:33.710
I believe came out in '97.

01:21:33.710 --> 01:21:35.240
'91, I'm sorry.

01:21:35.240 --> 01:21:37.160
That came out in '91.

01:21:37.160 --> 01:21:41.780
Thoughts about that?

01:21:41.780 --> 01:21:45.490
AUDIENCE: I thought
comparing the last two

01:21:45.490 --> 01:21:49.630
films we watched, the sense
of agency in the third film

01:21:49.630 --> 01:21:51.970
was so much stronger just
because of the different

01:21:51.970 --> 01:21:54.340
narrator, somebody
of the community.

01:21:54.340 --> 01:21:59.660
And in the third film, they
had a different relationship

01:21:59.660 --> 01:22:04.835
with technology than
in Nanook Revisited,

01:22:04.835 --> 01:22:08.250
in which they were still kind
of passively watching this movie

01:22:08.250 --> 01:22:11.600
and criticizing, but still
watching, and laughing,

01:22:11.600 --> 01:22:13.010
and looking at the pictures.

01:22:13.010 --> 01:22:18.200
And then the third one,
they had a lot more control

01:22:18.200 --> 01:22:20.610
of the technology.

01:22:20.610 --> 01:22:21.610
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Right.

01:22:21.610 --> 01:22:25.630
So it's Intuit media producers
using this technology

01:22:25.630 --> 01:22:30.780
for their own ends
and constructing

01:22:30.780 --> 01:22:34.552
the media for the questions
that they're interested in.

01:22:34.552 --> 01:22:36.510
AUDIENCE: I think it's
really interesting how--

01:22:36.510 --> 01:22:38.927
I mean, maybe just the fragment
we watched, but she really

01:22:38.927 --> 01:22:43.232
does stress that it
isn't an Inuit film,

01:22:43.232 --> 01:22:44.940
and it's made in this
language, and there

01:22:44.940 --> 01:22:47.107
is more filming in that the
language, in the written

01:22:47.107 --> 01:22:48.960
language, brings the
community together,

01:22:48.960 --> 01:22:51.570
and these things come
up over and over again.

01:22:51.570 --> 01:22:54.430
If it was a documentary
about TV in Italy,

01:22:54.430 --> 01:22:57.630
people wouldn't be stressing
that it brings Italians

01:22:57.630 --> 01:23:00.510
together and it's filmed
in Italian because it would

01:23:00.510 --> 01:23:03.390
be like, of course, that's
what TV does, communicates

01:23:03.390 --> 01:23:06.090
messages and is usually
in the local language.

01:23:06.090 --> 01:23:11.010
So it signifies that
this intense relationship

01:23:11.010 --> 01:23:13.770
with the technological
or political significance

01:23:13.770 --> 01:23:16.980
and maybe the hopes
attached to this project.

01:23:16.980 --> 01:23:18.930
CHRIS BOEBEL: An
interesting analogy to that.

01:23:18.930 --> 01:23:23.190
In parts of Europe,
though, is the stress

01:23:23.190 --> 01:23:25.020
on having a national
film industry,

01:23:25.020 --> 01:23:27.240
in many countries, a
native, quote, unquote,

01:23:27.240 --> 01:23:29.160
"language film
industry," that are

01:23:29.160 --> 01:23:30.360
supported by the government.

01:23:30.360 --> 01:23:34.990
I mean you think about Eastern
European countries, Germany,

01:23:34.990 --> 01:23:37.080
I mean lots of
countries in Europe

01:23:37.080 --> 01:23:39.627
have a film industry because
the government intervenes

01:23:39.627 --> 01:23:41.460
to make sure that they
have a film industry.

01:23:41.460 --> 01:23:46.590
And it's for reasons of
language, and culture,

01:23:46.590 --> 01:23:49.560
and defining yourself
in opposition

01:23:49.560 --> 01:23:52.740
to these hegemonic film
industries like the America

01:23:52.740 --> 01:23:57.060
film industry and
increasingly even Bollywood.

01:23:57.060 --> 01:24:03.570
So I mean, I think
film often ends up

01:24:03.570 --> 01:24:09.973
carrying those kinds of weights
for communities, cultures.

01:24:09.973 --> 01:24:12.390
CHRISTINE WALLEY: But I think
what Catarina is getting at,

01:24:12.390 --> 01:24:15.900
I mean, this move towards
Inuit media making

01:24:15.900 --> 01:24:19.133
is also part of a particular
kind of social movement

01:24:19.133 --> 01:24:20.550
which gets talked
about, if you're

01:24:20.550 --> 01:24:22.970
interested in the recommended
reading for this class,

01:24:22.970 --> 01:24:24.720
there's an article by
Faye Ginsberg called

01:24:24.720 --> 01:24:27.640
Screen Memories, where she talks
about the work of filmmakers

01:24:27.640 --> 01:24:28.140
Zach Kunuk.

01:24:28.140 --> 01:24:31.920
And he's the person who did the
fiction film that I mentioned,

01:24:31.920 --> 01:24:33.750
Fast Runner, and
he's also the one

01:24:33.750 --> 01:24:36.510
who did the documentary about
climate change that just got

01:24:36.510 --> 01:24:38.093
made this year that
we're going to see

01:24:38.093 --> 01:24:40.740
a little later in the class.

01:24:40.740 --> 01:24:43.680
And what Faye
Ginsberg argues there

01:24:43.680 --> 01:24:48.390
is that for many Indigenous
groups in different parts

01:24:48.390 --> 01:24:50.700
of the world, that
often the discussion

01:24:50.700 --> 01:24:54.180
around media technologies is
that this is modern technology

01:24:54.180 --> 01:24:55.680
doing things to them.

01:24:55.680 --> 01:24:58.860
And she talks about how,
for many Indigenous groups,

01:24:58.860 --> 01:25:02.070
they talk about both the
power that outside media

01:25:02.070 --> 01:25:04.952
representations can have to do
things like encourage language

01:25:04.952 --> 01:25:06.660
loss within particular
communities, which

01:25:06.660 --> 01:25:07.890
is a real issue here.

01:25:07.890 --> 01:25:09.600
But she also argues
that how many

01:25:09.600 --> 01:25:13.950
groups have mobilized around
using media for their own ends,

01:25:13.950 --> 01:25:15.792
for purposes of
cultural revitalization.

01:25:15.792 --> 01:25:17.250
And so it's very
interesting if you

01:25:17.250 --> 01:25:21.660
see a film like Fast Runner,
that film by Zach Kunuk,

01:25:21.660 --> 01:25:25.170
it's a fiction film that's
based on a 16th century myth.

01:25:25.170 --> 01:25:27.510
And it's kind of like
Flaherty's in the sense

01:25:27.510 --> 01:25:31.500
that it's meant to be part
of this pre-European contact

01:25:31.500 --> 01:25:32.592
portrait of Inuit society.

01:25:32.592 --> 01:25:34.300
But it's done for very
different reasons.

01:25:34.300 --> 01:25:36.383
So it's very interesting
to see them back-to-back,

01:25:36.383 --> 01:25:39.000
because they're both kind
of historical nostalgia

01:25:39.000 --> 01:25:40.890
pieces in a way, but
for different ends.

01:25:40.890 --> 01:25:43.320
Whereas Flaherty's
historical nostalgia

01:25:43.320 --> 01:25:46.560
is more a European
romantic primitivist idea,

01:25:46.560 --> 01:25:50.160
and on the Inuit end it's
kind of a romantic historical

01:25:50.160 --> 01:25:52.920
nostalgia piece,
but for the purposes

01:25:52.920 --> 01:25:57.060
of cultural revitalization
among Inuit peoples themselves,

01:25:57.060 --> 01:26:02.138
and to pull people together
both socially and politically.

01:26:02.138 --> 01:26:03.930
So they're interesting
to see back-to-back.

01:26:03.930 --> 01:26:05.820
So I would encourage
anybody who's

01:26:05.820 --> 01:26:07.860
interested in these
issues and have

01:26:07.860 --> 01:26:10.350
time-- which I know all of
you have so much extra time.

01:26:10.350 --> 01:26:11.850
CHRIS BOEBEL: In
terms of portrayal,

01:26:11.850 --> 01:26:13.642
one interesting thing
about the Fast Runner

01:26:13.642 --> 01:26:17.100
is that, like Nanook,
you have people dressed

01:26:17.100 --> 01:26:23.160
in traditional Inuit garb,
hunting in traditional ways,

01:26:23.160 --> 01:26:26.250
and as Chris said, it's
set in the 16th century.

01:26:26.250 --> 01:26:28.620
But at the end
during the credits,

01:26:28.620 --> 01:26:32.100
you see all of the actors
dressing contemporary garb,

01:26:32.100 --> 01:26:33.600
riding snowmobiles--

01:26:33.600 --> 01:26:34.270
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Black leather jackets.

01:26:34.270 --> 01:26:36.020
CHRIS BOEBEL: --and
black leather jackets.

01:26:36.020 --> 01:26:38.430
And the whole point
of that is to make

01:26:38.430 --> 01:26:43.500
clear to anybody who might be
remotely confused, that this

01:26:43.500 --> 01:26:49.080
was a fiction film, these were
actors, there were costumes,

01:26:49.080 --> 01:26:54.150
there were sets, and
it's not real life.

01:26:54.150 --> 01:26:58.568
It's not this eternal
culture, Inuit culture.

01:26:58.568 --> 01:27:00.110
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
So just to conclude

01:27:00.110 --> 01:27:01.880
our discussion of
Flaherty, because we

01:27:01.880 --> 01:27:04.880
need to move on to Vertov.

01:27:04.880 --> 01:27:08.000
So Flaherty, again, has been
this incredibly influential

01:27:08.000 --> 01:27:11.000
figure within documentary film.

01:27:11.000 --> 01:27:14.870
But there's also been a
lot of more recent debate

01:27:14.870 --> 01:27:17.450
about his work, partly based
on some of the things that we

01:27:17.450 --> 01:27:19.880
talked about, what
kinds of representations

01:27:19.880 --> 01:27:25.370
were being constructed, what
about the stuff that was

01:27:25.370 --> 01:27:28.560
faked in the film, and how
do people think about that?

01:27:28.560 --> 01:27:30.590
And I think some of
those historical debates

01:27:30.590 --> 01:27:31.977
have kind of--

01:27:31.977 --> 01:27:33.935
some of those debates
have died down a bit now.

01:27:37.220 --> 01:27:39.030
Basically, you
had the two sides.

01:27:39.030 --> 01:27:41.820
One side that was celebrating
the work of Flaherty

01:27:41.820 --> 01:27:43.820
and the other side was
the more revisionist side

01:27:43.820 --> 01:27:48.920
that said, look, there are
also quite problematic aspects

01:27:48.920 --> 01:27:50.070
of these representations.

01:27:50.070 --> 01:27:51.960
And I think now at
this point, the idea

01:27:51.960 --> 01:27:56.150
is more that it's a
bit of both, that it

01:27:56.150 --> 01:27:58.790
was a very innovative and
important piece of work

01:27:58.790 --> 01:28:00.840
for a variety of reasons.

01:28:00.840 --> 01:28:03.847
And it also has remained
important to Inuit people

01:28:03.847 --> 01:28:05.930
themselves again because
of its historical record.

01:28:05.930 --> 01:28:07.347
At the same time,
it was very much

01:28:07.347 --> 01:28:09.590
coming out of a particular
historical context that

01:28:09.590 --> 01:28:14.150
very much did feed into European
romantic primitivist ideas

01:28:14.150 --> 01:28:14.820
at the time.

01:28:14.820 --> 01:28:19.500
So it's a complicated piece
that has some of both going on.

01:28:19.500 --> 01:28:21.500
Any final thoughts that
any of you had before we

01:28:21.500 --> 01:28:24.630
move on to our
discussion of our Vertov.

01:28:24.630 --> 01:28:28.830
So now we're going
back to the 1920s.

01:28:28.830 --> 01:28:32.390
So this film, Man With a
Movie Camera came out in 1929.

01:28:32.390 --> 01:28:35.330
So it's slightly later than the
Flaherty Nanook of the North.

01:28:35.330 --> 01:28:39.632
It's an extremely different film
in almost every kind of way.

01:28:39.632 --> 01:28:41.090
It was made by a
Russian filmmaker,

01:28:41.090 --> 01:28:46.640
Dziga Vertov, who was born
in 1896, died in 1954.

01:28:46.640 --> 01:28:48.330
His name was a pseudonym.

01:28:48.330 --> 01:28:49.730
It meant spinning top.

01:28:49.730 --> 01:28:52.430
His actual name was Dennis
Kaufman, original name.

01:28:52.430 --> 01:28:55.640
He is born as a son of
Jewish intellectuals

01:28:55.640 --> 01:28:57.835
in an outlying region of Russia.

01:28:57.835 --> 01:28:59.210
And as you know
from the reading,

01:28:59.210 --> 01:29:01.280
Vertov started out
editing newsreels

01:29:01.280 --> 01:29:03.380
for the new Soviet
government right

01:29:03.380 --> 01:29:06.140
after it was formed, when
he was in his early 20s.

01:29:06.140 --> 01:29:08.570
And he made many films
later on with his wife,

01:29:08.570 --> 01:29:10.190
Elizaveta Svilova--

01:29:10.190 --> 01:29:12.590
sorry, I can't
pronounce it correctly--

01:29:12.590 --> 01:29:15.070
and his brother,
Mikhail Kaufman.

01:29:15.070 --> 01:29:16.820
And often in his
writings referred to them

01:29:16.820 --> 01:29:18.410
as the Council of
three, and they

01:29:18.410 --> 01:29:21.350
issued a number of
manifestos about film

01:29:21.350 --> 01:29:23.510
and about a new kind
of film journalism

01:29:23.510 --> 01:29:28.070
that they were arguing was
about capturing the, quote,

01:29:28.070 --> 01:29:29.462
unquote, "prose of life."

01:29:29.462 --> 01:29:31.670
He was an impassioned believer
in the ability of film

01:29:31.670 --> 01:29:33.253
to allow people to
look at their world

01:29:33.253 --> 01:29:37.700
in new ways, which you've
seen as manifestos.

01:29:37.700 --> 01:29:39.410
And it was also,
he saw it as part

01:29:39.410 --> 01:29:43.640
of this kind of thinking
about the world in new ways

01:29:43.640 --> 01:29:46.123
is also for him being
associated with the political

01:29:46.123 --> 01:29:47.540
transformations
that he associated

01:29:47.540 --> 01:29:51.410
with the Russian
revolution at the time.

01:29:51.410 --> 01:29:55.638
Later on, he became involved
in more feature length films.

01:29:55.638 --> 01:29:57.680
And he also developed an
increasingly complicated

01:29:57.680 --> 01:30:00.212
relationship to the
Soviet government,

01:30:00.212 --> 01:30:01.670
particularly under
Stalin as things

01:30:01.670 --> 01:30:04.310
became more bureaucratized.

01:30:04.310 --> 01:30:08.900
Vertov was a very independent
minded artist and filmmaker,

01:30:08.900 --> 01:30:14.030
and he was increasingly
seen as being anti planning

01:30:14.030 --> 01:30:19.430
and marginalized within
Soviet filmmaking.

01:30:19.430 --> 01:30:22.760
But despite his
marginalization over time

01:30:22.760 --> 01:30:25.700
within Soviet filmmaking,
he's had an enormous influence

01:30:25.700 --> 01:30:28.310
on European filmmakers,
particularly avant garde

01:30:28.310 --> 01:30:33.620
filmmakers, who still consider
this to be a classic film.

01:30:33.620 --> 01:30:35.060
And it's this film
in particular,

01:30:35.060 --> 01:30:39.285
Man With a Movie Camera, that's
most widely known of his work.

01:30:39.285 --> 01:30:40.910
So this film is a
dawn to dusk portrait

01:30:40.910 --> 01:30:42.982
of an archetypal Soviet city.

01:30:42.982 --> 01:30:45.440
And it was actually shot in
three different cities, Moscow,

01:30:45.440 --> 01:30:47.390
Kyiv, and Odessa.

01:30:47.390 --> 01:30:51.500
A couple of questions that you
should be thinking about as you

01:30:51.500 --> 01:30:54.200
watch this film.

01:30:54.200 --> 01:30:56.840
What is the particular vision
of filmmaking that Vertov is

01:30:56.840 --> 01:30:58.310
trying to get at in this film?

01:30:58.310 --> 01:31:00.650
And so he put out
a newsletter called

01:31:00.650 --> 01:31:02.270
Kino Pravda or Film Truth.

01:31:02.270 --> 01:31:04.370
So this question of,
what was film truth

01:31:04.370 --> 01:31:06.212
to Vertov What did
he mean by this term?

01:31:06.212 --> 01:31:08.420
What was he trying to capture
through his documentary

01:31:08.420 --> 01:31:11.240
filmmaking work?

01:31:11.240 --> 01:31:14.390
And also, he had a
very strong vision

01:31:14.390 --> 01:31:16.700
of science and technology.

01:31:16.700 --> 01:31:19.550
How does that come
out in the film?

01:31:19.550 --> 01:31:22.860
What's the portrayals of science
and technology in this piece?

01:31:22.860 --> 01:31:24.320
And then the third
question being,

01:31:24.320 --> 01:31:27.140
how does this film
compare with Nanook?

01:31:27.140 --> 01:31:29.900
Because again, it's an
extremely different style

01:31:29.900 --> 01:31:32.420
than Robert Flaherty's piece.

01:31:32.420 --> 01:31:34.460
If we think of the
two of them together,

01:31:34.460 --> 01:31:40.460
both coming out of the
context of the '20s, what

01:31:40.460 --> 01:31:45.437
do those contrasting styles
potentially suggest for us?

01:31:45.437 --> 01:31:48.431
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:51:13.596 --> 01:51:16.050
[END PLAYBACK]

01:51:16.050 --> 01:51:23.400
OK, so you see at work
doing a variety of things.

01:51:23.400 --> 01:51:26.100
You see different
life events or you

01:51:26.100 --> 01:51:28.800
see babies being born
in a hospital, people

01:51:28.800 --> 01:51:34.980
getting married, people in
divorce court, a funeral.

01:51:34.980 --> 01:51:37.920
Towards the end of the day
when the sun starts going down,

01:51:37.920 --> 01:51:40.680
you see recreation people
going out to sports events

01:51:40.680 --> 01:51:43.320
or to other kinds
of entertainment.

01:51:43.320 --> 01:51:45.990
And then the last piece
we want to show you--

01:51:45.990 --> 01:51:48.984
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:58:11.717 --> 01:58:13.930
[END PLAYBACK]

01:58:13.930 --> 01:58:16.460
Again, you haven't gotten
to see all of this film.

01:58:16.460 --> 01:58:19.575
But what is it about?

01:58:19.575 --> 01:58:23.430
AUDIENCE: It's about different--

01:58:23.430 --> 01:58:26.610
so what I got out of
it is that it's, like,

01:58:26.610 --> 01:58:31.410
trying to say that technology
is like stagnant without people,

01:58:31.410 --> 01:58:33.840
and people are stagnant
without technology.

01:58:33.840 --> 01:58:41.310
But film has, it's like, on its
own that people can embrace.

01:58:41.310 --> 01:58:44.580
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah,
so it's making an argument

01:58:44.580 --> 01:58:49.740
about film and technology, a
bunch of different arguments.

01:58:49.740 --> 01:58:53.044
Other expansions on
that, or thoughts?

01:58:53.044 --> 01:58:55.652
AUDIENCE: Questioning
[INAUDIBLE] manipulation.

01:58:55.652 --> 01:58:57.360
But it's not like he's
trying to hide it.

01:58:57.360 --> 01:58:59.957
He's trying to show you
what the camera can do.

01:58:59.957 --> 01:59:02.290
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Trying to
show what the camera can do.

01:59:05.400 --> 01:59:08.600
Is there a narrative
to this film?

01:59:08.600 --> 01:59:10.610
AUDIENCE: A day in the life?

01:59:10.610 --> 01:59:12.230
I mean, not quite,
but it does show

01:59:12.230 --> 01:59:13.560
from the beginning
where there's nobody out

01:59:13.560 --> 01:59:14.893
and then lots of people are out.

01:59:14.893 --> 01:59:18.500
And then people sitting down
watching a movie at the end.

01:59:20.442 --> 01:59:21.400
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

01:59:21.400 --> 01:59:23.740
So you don't-- and again,
because you only saw a piece

01:59:23.740 --> 01:59:25.520
of it, it might be
a little less clear.

01:59:25.520 --> 01:59:28.120
But again, it is supposed
to be this dawn to dusk

01:59:28.120 --> 01:59:29.128
in an archetypal city.

01:59:29.128 --> 01:59:31.420
And again, it's images from
three different cities that

01:59:31.420 --> 01:59:33.310
are edited together
to make it appear

01:59:33.310 --> 01:59:36.610
as if it's like this
quintessential Soviet city.

01:59:36.610 --> 01:59:38.628
So you see people waking
up at the beginning

01:59:38.628 --> 01:59:39.670
and then they go to work.

01:59:39.670 --> 01:59:42.280
And there's a lot of stuff in
the middle about people working

01:59:42.280 --> 01:59:49.180
and watching technology
and machines move as part

01:59:49.180 --> 01:59:50.385
of everyday kinds of work.

01:59:50.385 --> 01:59:51.760
And then, as I
mentioned, there's

01:59:51.760 --> 01:59:53.427
all these different
kinds of life events

01:59:53.427 --> 01:59:54.610
that you're shown going on.

01:59:54.610 --> 01:59:57.152
And then towards the end of the
day, people are done working.

02:00:00.670 --> 02:00:02.530
You see various
recreation scenes, people

02:00:02.530 --> 02:00:03.880
going to sporting events.

02:00:03.880 --> 02:00:07.690
And then at the end,
they go to the cinema.

02:00:07.690 --> 02:00:09.610
And they watch this film.

02:00:09.610 --> 02:00:11.560
So there's one loose
plot line that's

02:00:11.560 --> 02:00:16.570
about the dawn to dusk in the
life of an archetypal city.

02:00:16.570 --> 02:00:19.120
And then intertwined
with that, you

02:00:19.120 --> 02:00:23.470
have this thread
about the filmmaker.

02:00:23.470 --> 02:00:27.390
So what's the thread
about the filmmaker?

02:00:27.390 --> 02:00:29.890
AUDIENCE: It was interesting
that it starts with this camera

02:00:29.890 --> 02:00:30.783
man on a camera.

02:00:30.783 --> 02:00:31.950
CHRISTINE WALLEY: I'm sorry?

02:00:31.950 --> 02:00:35.170
AUDIENCE: The scene, I mean, the
film starts with the camera--

02:00:35.170 --> 02:00:36.143
two cameras.

02:00:36.143 --> 02:00:38.060
One camera man with the
camera and the camera.

02:00:38.060 --> 02:00:40.240
And it carries on
throughout the movie,

02:00:40.240 --> 02:00:43.720
the idea of the eye watching
the eye or the camera watching

02:00:43.720 --> 02:00:46.570
the camera, and then people
watching the camera that

02:00:46.570 --> 02:00:48.910
shoot the camera.

02:00:48.910 --> 02:00:51.310
It's kind of a
chain of the camera

02:00:51.310 --> 02:00:53.440
or the eyes looking
at each other.

02:00:53.440 --> 02:00:59.350
And the aperture of the eye
and the curtain opening,

02:00:59.350 --> 02:01:01.270
it was just like, [INAUDIBLE].

02:01:01.270 --> 02:01:07.210
And motion pictures
versus static pictures

02:01:07.210 --> 02:01:08.240
in the beginning.

02:01:08.240 --> 02:01:10.510
It was also interesting
because the music,

02:01:10.510 --> 02:01:13.540
the minimalist music,
the progression of starts

02:01:13.540 --> 02:01:18.490
slow and then the editing was
very with the-- and with music.

02:01:18.490 --> 02:01:23.080
I think it was new in this
one rather like the other ones

02:01:23.080 --> 02:01:24.620
that we watched before that.

02:01:24.620 --> 02:01:26.450
CHRIS BOEBEL: So the
music just interrupt,

02:01:26.450 --> 02:01:29.132
but the music, this would
have been shown with music.

02:01:29.132 --> 02:01:31.090
But the music would have
varied and it wouldn't

02:01:31.090 --> 02:01:33.190
have been exactly this music.

02:01:33.190 --> 02:01:36.040
CHRISTINE WALLEY: But
I believe Vertov did--

02:01:36.040 --> 02:01:39.370
I mean, he started out early
on doing avant garde sound

02:01:39.370 --> 02:01:39.970
compositions.

02:01:39.970 --> 02:01:41.470
And I believe that
this was actually

02:01:41.470 --> 02:01:42.605
a reconstruction movie.

02:01:42.605 --> 02:01:44.980
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, and it's
inspired by it or something,

02:01:44.980 --> 02:01:45.480
yeah.

02:01:45.480 --> 02:01:46.030
But I mean--

02:01:46.030 --> 02:01:47.350
AUDIENCE: The music
wasn't original?

02:01:47.350 --> 02:01:49.600
CHRIS BOEBEL: The music, I
mean this is a silent film,

02:01:49.600 --> 02:01:51.310
but they would be played--

02:01:51.310 --> 02:01:54.040
when these films were
shown, this one or Nanook,

02:01:54.040 --> 02:01:56.200
there would have been an
orchestra or some kind

02:01:56.200 --> 02:01:57.640
of musical accompaniment.

02:01:57.640 --> 02:02:02.860
And I think that this
soundtrack is inspired

02:02:02.860 --> 02:02:05.590
by or a reconstruction
of the kinds of things

02:02:05.590 --> 02:02:09.400
that Vertov experimented
with musically.

02:02:09.400 --> 02:02:14.030
So it is a separate thing.

02:02:14.030 --> 02:02:16.480
AUDIENCE: It wasn't
based on the music.

02:02:16.480 --> 02:02:19.983
CHRIS BOEBEL: It's a little bit
of a not quite 1 to 1 ratio.

02:02:19.983 --> 02:02:21.400
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
So the music was

02:02:21.400 --> 02:02:24.550
done by the composer Michael
Nyman, who did research

02:02:24.550 --> 02:02:29.680
on the period, the incredible
artistic creativity that

02:02:29.680 --> 02:02:31.880
immediately followed
the Russian Revolution.

02:02:31.880 --> 02:02:34.900
And so it's based on
research imagining

02:02:34.900 --> 02:02:38.270
what music might have been.

02:02:38.270 --> 02:02:41.390
AUDIENCE: But I think
they were very closely

02:02:41.390 --> 02:02:42.308
related, weren't they?

02:02:42.308 --> 02:02:43.100
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah.

02:02:43.100 --> 02:02:46.040
Well, the editing
is kind of stunning.

02:02:46.040 --> 02:02:51.350
I mean, if you think
about the time period

02:02:51.350 --> 02:02:54.740
and then compare
that to Nanook, it's

02:02:54.740 --> 02:02:57.710
just a completely different
relationship to film,

02:02:57.710 --> 02:02:59.720
a completely different
relationship to editing,

02:02:59.720 --> 02:03:02.522
and as people have said,
what the camera can do.

02:03:02.522 --> 02:03:03.980
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
But before we get

02:03:03.980 --> 02:03:06.330
into comparison of
Nanook and Vertov,

02:03:06.330 --> 02:03:09.140
because I think that's where
we want this to go, but first

02:03:09.140 --> 02:03:11.480
can we get out a bit
more a bit about Vertov

02:03:11.480 --> 02:03:12.560
and what's going on here?

02:03:12.560 --> 02:03:14.060
So we have the
parallels as you note

02:03:14.060 --> 02:03:17.180
between the human
eye and the camera

02:03:17.180 --> 02:03:20.190
eye going back and forth,
making comparisons between them.

02:03:20.190 --> 02:03:23.820
And what kind of
argument is he making?

02:03:23.820 --> 02:03:26.696
Do you guys have a--

02:03:26.696 --> 02:03:29.570
AUDIENCE: There's a bit of a
danger in like, I mean, he's

02:03:29.570 --> 02:03:31.820
climbing, like, a bridge
over where the train's coming

02:03:31.820 --> 02:03:33.612
in and then also going
underneath the train

02:03:33.612 --> 02:03:36.520
track and stuff.

02:03:36.520 --> 02:03:38.040
And as soon as like
the train looks

02:03:38.040 --> 02:03:39.623
like it might have
run him over, like,

02:03:39.623 --> 02:03:41.592
the girl wakes up, like
something has gone on.

02:03:41.592 --> 02:03:43.967
I don't know if she's, like,
related to him or something,

02:03:43.967 --> 02:03:46.220
supposedly, but yeah.

02:03:46.220 --> 02:03:48.050
AUDIENCE: It's so beautiful.

02:03:51.320 --> 02:03:57.570
And I mean, it's incredible
how the camera has

02:03:57.570 --> 02:04:00.990
a life on its own, the
cameraman is trying to--

02:04:00.990 --> 02:04:03.030
you have the cameraman
trying to film

02:04:03.030 --> 02:04:06.180
what the camera wants to film.

02:04:06.180 --> 02:04:07.820
And it's never really there.

02:04:07.820 --> 02:04:10.500
It's always the climbing,
and making the effort,

02:04:10.500 --> 02:04:12.780
and the camera is
showing all these things

02:04:12.780 --> 02:04:16.840
that you would never be able
to see unmediated by the camera

02:04:16.840 --> 02:04:19.000
and unmediated by
the process of making

02:04:19.000 --> 02:04:23.040
the film that shows the kind
of montage and screening.

02:04:26.060 --> 02:04:27.357
It's really dazzling.

02:04:27.357 --> 02:04:28.550
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Mm-hmm.

02:04:28.550 --> 02:04:31.070
So a lot of different
threads going on.

02:04:31.070 --> 02:04:41.390
Maybe one place to start
in terms of Vertov,

02:04:41.390 --> 02:04:43.810
one thing that you might have
gotten from your readings

02:04:43.810 --> 02:04:46.210
was that he was
heavily influenced

02:04:46.210 --> 02:04:48.790
by the futurist
movement in Europe.

02:04:48.790 --> 02:04:50.530
So the futurist
movement coming out

02:04:50.530 --> 02:04:54.610
of being inspired partly
by Italian futurism,

02:04:54.610 --> 02:04:58.325
French cubism, any sense of what
those artistic movements were

02:04:58.325 --> 02:04:58.825
about?

02:05:02.163 --> 02:05:04.080
AUDIENCE: So there are
a bunch of young male--

02:05:04.080 --> 02:05:04.955
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:05:04.955 --> 02:05:05.970
[LAUGHTER]

02:05:05.970 --> 02:05:10.050
AUDIENCE: In Italy in the '20s,
and they travel a lot in Paris

02:05:10.050 --> 02:05:11.760
to see what the
Cubists are up to.

02:05:11.760 --> 02:05:13.980
But the Cubists are
mainly interested in space

02:05:13.980 --> 02:05:16.920
and in how emptiness is
full and bring it up.

02:05:16.920 --> 02:05:19.380
But the futurists are
interested in movement.

02:05:19.380 --> 02:05:23.310
And this is why the Cubists only
painted like brown and white

02:05:23.310 --> 02:05:25.140
and the futurists
paint with colors,

02:05:25.140 --> 02:05:27.390
because colors is
associated with movement

02:05:27.390 --> 02:05:29.490
and movement is
associated with what

02:05:29.490 --> 02:05:31.020
is cool things the
young males like,

02:05:31.020 --> 02:05:34.890
like sports, fast cars,
war, Italy at the time

02:05:34.890 --> 02:05:37.740
is trying to have a couple
of colonies in Africa.

02:05:37.740 --> 02:05:40.553
And these guys are
really excited about it.

02:05:40.553 --> 02:05:41.970
They're excited
about first place,

02:05:41.970 --> 02:05:43.560
they're excited
about breaking down

02:05:43.560 --> 02:05:46.950
the land which we have this
future manifesto where they

02:05:46.950 --> 02:05:51.870
develop a way of writing
without giving up punctuation,

02:05:51.870 --> 02:05:55.020
like rules of punctuation
and rules of plot.

02:05:55.020 --> 02:05:58.890
And there is a lot of
communication between Paris,

02:05:58.890 --> 02:06:00.250
Italy, and Russia.

02:06:00.250 --> 02:06:01.517
A lot of back and forth.

02:06:01.517 --> 02:06:02.850
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, exactly.

02:06:02.850 --> 02:06:06.150
So part of the influence on
Vertov of the futurist movement

02:06:06.150 --> 02:06:08.370
would be being a fascination
with the future, which

02:06:08.370 --> 02:06:11.850
was very much associated with
the possibilities of technology

02:06:11.850 --> 02:06:14.400
as they were seen by
at least some people

02:06:14.400 --> 02:06:15.820
during this time period.

02:06:15.820 --> 02:06:20.790
So a fascination with machinery,
modern technologies of various

02:06:20.790 --> 02:06:24.330
kinds, a fascination
with movement,

02:06:24.330 --> 02:06:29.590
a fascination linked to both of
those about what can the camera

02:06:29.590 --> 02:06:33.150
eye do differently or allow
us to see about the world that

02:06:33.150 --> 02:06:35.818
the human eye can't?

02:06:35.818 --> 02:06:37.860
So this is where a lot of
Vertov was coming from.

02:06:37.860 --> 02:06:39.610
And like some of the
other futurists where

02:06:39.610 --> 02:06:41.010
he wrote a lot of manifestos--

02:06:41.010 --> 02:06:42.510
for those of you
who are interested,

02:06:42.510 --> 02:06:43.830
it's on the website.

02:06:43.830 --> 02:06:48.150
But just to read you a bit of
one of those where he writes,

02:06:48.150 --> 02:06:51.210
"I am kino eye,"
meaning cinema eye.

02:06:51.210 --> 02:06:52.920
"I am a mechanical eye.

02:06:52.920 --> 02:06:55.770
I am machine show you the
world as only I can see it.

02:06:55.770 --> 02:06:59.070
Now and forever, I free
myself from human immobility.

02:06:59.070 --> 02:07:00.660
I am in constant motion.

02:07:00.660 --> 02:07:02.700
I draw near then
away from objects.

02:07:02.700 --> 02:07:04.980
I crawl under, I
climb onto them.

02:07:04.980 --> 02:07:06.810
I move space with
the muzzle of--

02:07:06.810 --> 02:07:08.490
I'm sorry-- I move
apace with the muzzle

02:07:08.490 --> 02:07:09.630
of a galloping horse.

02:07:09.630 --> 02:07:11.580
I plunge full
speed into a crowd.

02:07:11.580 --> 02:07:13.290
I outstrip running soldiers.

02:07:13.290 --> 02:07:14.397
I fall on my back.

02:07:14.397 --> 02:07:15.480
I ascend with an airplane.

02:07:15.480 --> 02:07:18.750
I plunge and soar together with
plunging and soaring bodies."

02:07:18.750 --> 02:07:22.470
And he goes on and on
celebrating the movement,

02:07:22.470 --> 02:07:24.870
the technology, the camera
eye, that the camera is

02:07:24.870 --> 02:07:27.683
superior to the human eye.

02:07:27.683 --> 02:07:29.100
CHRIS BOEBEL:
Directly contradicts

02:07:29.100 --> 02:07:31.247
what I said in the
first class, but it's

02:07:31.247 --> 02:07:32.955
coming from a very
different perspective.

02:07:32.955 --> 02:07:33.210
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Debatable point.

02:07:33.210 --> 02:07:33.900
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah.

02:07:33.900 --> 02:07:34.140
Yeah.

02:07:34.140 --> 02:07:36.240
CHRISTINE WALLEY: But
similar to some of what

02:07:36.240 --> 02:07:39.000
was discussed in last week's
class, a fascination, well,

02:07:39.000 --> 02:07:42.840
what can you see differently
with the camera lens

02:07:42.840 --> 02:07:45.480
than you would, potentially,
with the human eye?

02:07:45.480 --> 02:07:47.250
And the human eye--
and a lot of people

02:07:47.250 --> 02:07:49.083
have critiqued Vertov
for this, that there's

02:07:49.083 --> 02:07:51.600
this incredible celebration
of machinery and technology,

02:07:51.600 --> 02:07:53.370
but the human eye is amazing.

02:07:53.370 --> 02:07:57.060
That actually, the camera
lens can't do a lot of things

02:07:57.060 --> 02:07:58.875
that the human eye
is capable of doing.

02:07:58.875 --> 02:08:00.750
But there are some things
that the camera can

02:08:00.750 --> 02:08:03.910
do that the human eye can't do.

02:08:03.910 --> 02:08:07.560
And so last week we saw some
of the microcinematography,

02:08:07.560 --> 02:08:10.650
the time lapse, the
fascinating perspective

02:08:10.650 --> 02:08:13.710
you get by doing
time lapse filmmaking

02:08:13.710 --> 02:08:15.280
to see things in a new way.

02:08:15.280 --> 02:08:18.600
And so here, Vertov's
fascination with, how can we

02:08:18.600 --> 02:08:22.232
see using the camera lens?

02:08:22.232 --> 02:08:23.940
And so as part of
that, you get this kind

02:08:23.940 --> 02:08:26.790
of heroic portrait
of the filmmaker

02:08:26.790 --> 02:08:29.280
as this hero that's going to
get out there and get people

02:08:29.280 --> 02:08:31.230
to think differently
using the camera

02:08:31.230 --> 02:08:34.170
eye from all these
different kinds of angles.

02:08:34.170 --> 02:08:35.700
That you can capture
things in time,

02:08:35.700 --> 02:08:37.127
see it at another
time period, you

02:08:37.127 --> 02:08:39.210
could get perspectives on
things that you wouldn't

02:08:39.210 --> 02:08:45.060
be able to see before,
you can have the filmmaker

02:08:45.060 --> 02:08:48.930
climb under the train in a pit
and capture that angle, which

02:08:48.930 --> 02:08:51.480
the rest of us could
do if we did that.

02:08:51.480 --> 02:08:55.320
But the camera eye
allowing those kinds of

02:08:55.320 --> 02:08:59.040
perspectives to be
recorded and to be shared.

02:08:59.040 --> 02:09:01.420
And in addition to this,
the focus on editing.

02:09:01.420 --> 02:09:04.410
So what is the
editing like here?

02:09:04.410 --> 02:09:05.910
And how is it
different than what we

02:09:05.910 --> 02:09:09.130
saw in Flaherty, for example?

02:09:09.130 --> 02:09:09.880
Matt?

02:09:09.880 --> 02:09:13.030
AUDIENCE: I mean, he tried
to make the editing really

02:09:13.030 --> 02:09:15.010
profound at the same
time really obvious

02:09:15.010 --> 02:09:18.880
that he put a lot of effort into
making it profound by the fact

02:09:18.880 --> 02:09:20.320
that her he had
to expose himself.

02:09:20.320 --> 02:09:22.780
So you know the guy who's
actually doing the editing.

02:09:22.780 --> 02:09:24.820
And also, it shows
woman doing the editing.

02:09:24.820 --> 02:09:28.220
And then he does some
really ridiculous thing

02:09:28.220 --> 02:09:33.870
where they're doing the two
scene split in the upside down.

02:09:33.870 --> 02:09:38.675
So it's almost like
controlled chaos in a way.

02:09:38.675 --> 02:09:40.300
It was like he took
everything and then

02:09:40.300 --> 02:09:42.887
just put it all together
into something logical.

02:09:42.887 --> 02:09:43.720
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right.

02:09:43.720 --> 02:09:47.590
Whereas, I mean,
again, with Nanook,

02:09:47.590 --> 02:09:53.470
you're using editing to create
an illusion of the way we

02:09:53.470 --> 02:09:56.170
perceive reality with
our eyes and our brains,

02:09:56.170 --> 02:09:59.950
taking in a scene, close ups,
establishing shots, pans.

02:09:59.950 --> 02:10:04.480
Here it's very much saying,
no, editing is its own thing.

02:10:04.480 --> 02:10:09.640
Editing for the sake of
editing is what filmmaking is.

02:10:09.640 --> 02:10:13.000
And this links up to
theories of montage

02:10:13.000 --> 02:10:15.008
which is, like,
sequences of shot, which

02:10:15.008 --> 02:10:16.300
were very big in Soviet cinema.

02:10:16.300 --> 02:10:20.550
But it's basically
saying, again, this

02:10:20.550 --> 02:10:23.050
is not the human eye, this is
not the human brain perceiving

02:10:23.050 --> 02:10:23.550
stuff.

02:10:23.550 --> 02:10:25.870
We're doing something,
in his opinion,

02:10:25.870 --> 02:10:29.350
better, something you can't do
with your eye and your brain.

02:10:29.350 --> 02:10:30.362
And here's how it works.

02:10:30.362 --> 02:10:31.820
I'm going to break
it down for you.

02:10:31.820 --> 02:10:34.330
I'm going to show you
the person doing it.

02:10:34.330 --> 02:10:36.340
Then I'm going to show
you how film works,

02:10:36.340 --> 02:10:38.740
stills going into motion.

02:10:38.740 --> 02:10:41.920
I'm going to show you what
happens when you take this shot

02:10:41.920 --> 02:10:44.170
and put it next to that
shot and how it changes

02:10:44.170 --> 02:10:45.880
when this shot is not
next to that shot,

02:10:45.880 --> 02:10:48.005
and how you reverse them,
and how you split screen,

02:10:48.005 --> 02:10:48.940
and what that does.

02:10:48.940 --> 02:10:52.547
I mean, it's really interesting.

02:10:52.547 --> 02:10:54.130
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
So just to follow up

02:10:54.130 --> 02:10:55.450
what Chris was saying.

02:10:55.450 --> 02:10:59.918
So montage, being strongly--

02:10:59.918 --> 02:11:01.460
a particular kind
of montage, anyway,

02:11:01.460 --> 02:11:05.390
being strongly associated
with early Soviet filmmaking.

02:11:05.390 --> 02:11:07.675
So how is meaning created then?

02:11:10.670 --> 02:11:11.200
Sorry?

02:11:11.200 --> 02:11:14.218
AUDIENCE: The juxtaposition
of images [INAUDIBLE]

02:11:14.218 --> 02:11:15.500
associations [INAUDIBLE].

02:11:15.500 --> 02:11:16.458
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

02:11:16.458 --> 02:11:19.820
And what kind of
images do you want--

02:11:19.820 --> 02:11:23.480
or what,
philosophically, are you

02:11:23.480 --> 02:11:26.120
trying to do when you're
juxtaposing these images?

02:11:26.120 --> 02:11:27.828
AUDIENCE: You're saying
they're connected

02:11:27.828 --> 02:11:31.540
and also depending
on how you edit it,

02:11:31.540 --> 02:11:33.845
it might be, like,
making it more glamorous

02:11:33.845 --> 02:11:35.745
or something, or
different things.

02:11:35.745 --> 02:11:37.912
In this case, it
was [INAUDIBLE]..

02:11:37.912 --> 02:11:38.870
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

02:11:38.870 --> 02:11:43.203
So again, unlike Flaherty,
where the editing

02:11:43.203 --> 02:11:44.870
would be to create a
sense of continuity

02:11:44.870 --> 02:11:47.060
and a sense of realism
and being in a space,

02:11:47.060 --> 02:11:49.940
here, the meaning, what is
the meaning created out of?

02:11:49.940 --> 02:11:52.370
It's taking things that are
completely different, putting

02:11:52.370 --> 02:11:53.888
them in relation to each other.

02:11:53.888 --> 02:11:56.180
And the idea being that if
you have something different

02:11:56.180 --> 02:11:57.805
over here, something
over here, and you

02:11:57.805 --> 02:12:00.710
put it and you jam it up
against the other thing,

02:12:00.710 --> 02:12:04.070
that it creates a new
kind of meaning by putting

02:12:04.070 --> 02:12:06.620
those things together.

02:12:06.620 --> 02:12:10.035
So the Soviet idea of
montage and meaning

02:12:10.035 --> 02:12:11.660
then coming out of
the fact that you're

02:12:11.660 --> 02:12:15.530
taking disparate elements and
by using the camera in this way,

02:12:15.530 --> 02:12:18.080
you're creating new ways of
seeing the world because you're

02:12:18.080 --> 02:12:19.970
juxtaposing images that
you wouldn't normally

02:12:19.970 --> 02:12:22.370
be juxtaposing in experience.

02:12:22.370 --> 02:12:24.530
But by juxtaposing them
in certain kinds of ways,

02:12:24.530 --> 02:12:26.990
you can make arguments,
you can express ideas,

02:12:26.990 --> 02:12:28.040
you create meaning.

02:12:28.040 --> 02:12:31.670
That's how you create meaning
is through the montage

02:12:31.670 --> 02:12:34.835
rather than creating meaning
through a sense of continuity,

02:12:34.835 --> 02:12:36.710
or a sense that you
actually were in a place,

02:12:36.710 --> 02:12:39.140
or that you're capturing
some kind of reality.

02:12:39.140 --> 02:12:42.650
OK so Kino Pravda or
film truth for Vertov

02:12:42.650 --> 02:12:46.840
means something very different
than the sense of truth in film

02:12:46.840 --> 02:12:49.670
might mean for Flaherty
or for other people.

02:12:49.670 --> 02:12:51.950
Here, it's very
much not the truth

02:12:51.950 --> 02:12:54.830
of trying to be realistic
to people's experiences

02:12:54.830 --> 02:12:57.770
of day-to-day life, but a sense
of being a certain kind of film

02:12:57.770 --> 02:12:59.940
truth where you're
trying to use the camera

02:12:59.940 --> 02:13:02.810
and what the camera can
do to get people to think

02:13:02.810 --> 02:13:06.110
about the world in a new
way, and according to him,

02:13:06.110 --> 02:13:09.800
kind of have some kind of truth
revelation about the world

02:13:09.800 --> 02:13:12.890
and how the world works
through these arguments that

02:13:12.890 --> 02:13:15.380
construct it through
the montage in the film.

02:13:15.380 --> 02:13:17.595
So it's a very particular
conception of film truth.

02:13:17.595 --> 02:13:19.220
And that's quite
different than the one

02:13:19.220 --> 02:13:21.260
that you would often hear
people talking about,

02:13:21.260 --> 02:13:23.468
say, in the United States
in relation to documentary.

02:13:23.468 --> 02:13:25.260
CHRIS BOEBEL: And one
way to think about it

02:13:25.260 --> 02:13:26.600
is in terms of manipulation.

02:13:26.600 --> 02:13:30.080
I mean, all film on one level
or another is a manipulation.

02:13:30.080 --> 02:13:33.980
And with the Flaherty
approach, the whole point

02:13:33.980 --> 02:13:35.840
is to hide the manipulation.

02:13:35.840 --> 02:13:37.910
All of the stuff we were
talking about in terms

02:13:37.910 --> 02:13:41.030
of the background, behind
the scenes, all of the things

02:13:41.030 --> 02:13:43.790
that were done, all of the
changes that were made,

02:13:43.790 --> 02:13:47.960
the repositioning of the
camera, the way it's edited,

02:13:47.960 --> 02:13:50.805
it's all designed to make you
forget about that as a viewer

02:13:50.805 --> 02:13:52.430
and just immerse
yourself in this world

02:13:52.430 --> 02:13:55.010
that he's constructed, even
though it's a documentary.

02:13:55.010 --> 02:13:58.250
Whereas here, the
manipulation is the point.

02:13:58.250 --> 02:13:59.990
It's all n your face.

02:13:59.990 --> 02:14:04.820
The whole point of it is to jam
it down your throat as a viewer

02:14:04.820 --> 02:14:06.440
and say, revel in it.

02:14:06.440 --> 02:14:07.320
This is exciting.

02:14:07.320 --> 02:14:10.370
This is what this art form
is about, this manipulation.

02:14:10.370 --> 02:14:11.828
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
And how does this

02:14:11.828 --> 02:14:16.040
relate to narrative, a sense
of narrative being a question?

02:14:16.040 --> 02:14:18.290
I mean again, the question,
what's the narrative here?

02:14:18.290 --> 02:14:21.770
I mean, we talked about
loosely there's certain themes.

02:14:21.770 --> 02:14:24.020
Whether we want to see it
as a fully fledged narrative

02:14:24.020 --> 02:14:25.020
might be something else.

02:14:25.020 --> 02:14:30.620
But one of the things that
Vertov and the Council of three

02:14:30.620 --> 02:14:33.200
very strongly argued for is
that they want to break--

02:14:33.200 --> 02:14:35.287
that this is about film
experiments and the idea

02:14:35.287 --> 02:14:37.370
is to completely break
with theater and literature

02:14:37.370 --> 02:14:39.980
and other kinds of
fiction filmmaking.

02:14:39.980 --> 02:14:41.720
So they're arguing
for a radical break,

02:14:41.720 --> 02:14:44.750
that they want to break with
narratives that you commonly

02:14:44.750 --> 02:14:48.650
found in Hollywood films
that were being made,

02:14:48.650 --> 02:14:52.010
starting to be made during
this period with literature,

02:14:52.010 --> 02:14:53.580
with theater, and
they're saying,

02:14:53.580 --> 02:14:55.288
no, we want to completely
get rid of that

02:14:55.288 --> 02:14:57.110
and do something different.

02:14:57.110 --> 02:15:01.790
And just to read a little
bit more of the manifesto,

02:15:01.790 --> 02:15:04.040
Vertov writes, "We proclaim
the old films based

02:15:04.040 --> 02:15:06.410
on the romance, theatrical
films, and the like

02:15:06.410 --> 02:15:08.390
to be leprous.

02:15:08.390 --> 02:15:11.660
Keep them away, exclamation
mark, keep your eyes off them.

02:15:11.660 --> 02:15:14.468
They're mortally dangerous,
contagious, exclamation mark."

02:15:14.468 --> 02:15:16.760
I mean, it's really fun to
read some of the manifestos.

02:15:16.760 --> 02:15:20.240
They're quite manifesto-like.

02:15:20.240 --> 02:15:23.360
"We affirm the future of cinema
art by denying its present.

02:15:23.360 --> 02:15:26.900
Cinematography must die so that
the art of cinema may live.

02:15:26.900 --> 02:15:28.880
We call for its death
to be hastened."

02:15:28.880 --> 02:15:31.712
So basically making a
very radical critique

02:15:31.712 --> 02:15:33.920
of other kinds of filmmaking
that would be out there,

02:15:33.920 --> 02:15:36.045
he would be making a very
radical critique of, say,

02:15:36.045 --> 02:15:38.780
Flaherty's style
of making film that

02:15:38.780 --> 02:15:42.590
has more continuities
with fiction filmmaking

02:15:42.590 --> 02:15:44.840
at the time and other
kinds of storytelling.

02:15:47.570 --> 02:15:50.210
What about the
relationship to people?

02:15:50.210 --> 02:15:53.030
So again, think about
Flaherty and Nanook

02:15:53.030 --> 02:15:55.025
of the North and this piece.

02:15:58.530 --> 02:16:00.810
What's the
relationship to people

02:16:00.810 --> 02:16:03.690
that gets conveyed
by the filmmaker

02:16:03.690 --> 02:16:04.920
in both of these pieces?

02:16:04.920 --> 02:16:08.010
How is it different?

02:16:08.010 --> 02:16:10.770
AUDIENCE: In Nanook where
like one family that you're

02:16:10.770 --> 02:16:13.050
kind of identifying
more specifically Nanook

02:16:13.050 --> 02:16:15.690
versus in this one, there's
no one person that you really

02:16:15.690 --> 02:16:16.710
follow too much.

02:16:16.710 --> 02:16:18.220
I mean, there's a little bit
but they cut back and forth

02:16:18.220 --> 02:16:19.832
to the same person,
but it's just

02:16:19.832 --> 02:16:22.040
like the whole population,
which is kind of, I guess,

02:16:22.040 --> 02:16:24.200
the Soviet culture
they're trying to--

02:16:24.200 --> 02:16:25.742
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Is it about people?

02:16:27.560 --> 02:16:30.830
AUDIENCE: There's no distinction
between people and, like,

02:16:30.830 --> 02:16:32.389
inanimate objects it seems.

02:16:32.389 --> 02:16:34.388
Like, they just--

02:16:34.388 --> 02:16:34.930
I don't know.

02:16:34.930 --> 02:16:37.270
Sometimes, like, in some of
the parts, there was, like,

02:16:37.270 --> 02:16:39.500
the girl doing
something that related,

02:16:39.500 --> 02:16:42.790
there was a parallel to
something, like, physical.

02:16:42.790 --> 02:16:48.250
And in the Nanook
story, it was all

02:16:48.250 --> 02:16:51.129
based on the relationship with
people, what they're doing,

02:16:51.129 --> 02:16:54.572
their interaction with
the world around them.

02:16:54.572 --> 02:16:55.530
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

02:16:55.530 --> 02:16:57.840
So in Nanook, so it's
about individuals

02:16:57.840 --> 02:16:59.940
following individual
stories, creating a narrative

02:16:59.940 --> 02:17:00.600
in that way.

02:17:00.600 --> 02:17:02.827
And here, to the extent--

02:17:02.827 --> 02:17:04.660
I mean, certainly there
are people in there.

02:17:04.660 --> 02:17:07.260
But it's more of a visual
representation of society,

02:17:07.260 --> 02:17:09.100
in a sense, if you want
to see it that way.

02:17:09.100 --> 02:17:11.850
So the idea is not to capture
individuals and follow them

02:17:11.850 --> 02:17:13.709
in their experiences,
but to convey

02:17:13.709 --> 02:17:17.820
some kind of more abstract idea
of what, say, Soviet society

02:17:17.820 --> 02:17:21.719
would be and to see
its pieces as working,

02:17:21.719 --> 02:17:26.430
as being about movement, maybe
about the interdependencies

02:17:26.430 --> 02:17:28.320
and the division
of labor where you

02:17:28.320 --> 02:17:31.230
see different categories
of people doing

02:17:31.230 --> 02:17:32.612
different kinds of things.

02:17:32.612 --> 02:17:34.320
There's also a critique
implicit in here.

02:17:34.320 --> 02:17:35.610
For those of you who read--

02:17:35.610 --> 02:17:37.860
you might have read some of
the background information

02:17:37.860 --> 02:17:39.750
but others you wouldn't.

02:17:39.750 --> 02:17:41.940
So immediately after
the Russian Revolution,

02:17:41.940 --> 02:17:46.770
there was a lot of creative
dynamic stuff happening, also

02:17:46.770 --> 02:17:49.590
very difficult
times economically.

02:17:49.590 --> 02:17:51.840
And so Lenin, at
a certain point,

02:17:51.840 --> 02:17:54.330
instituted the new
economic program

02:17:54.330 --> 02:17:58.770
which was a bit of a
retreat from the revolution

02:17:58.770 --> 02:17:59.590
at that point.

02:17:59.590 --> 02:18:05.879
And so it was more allowing
more capitalist relations

02:18:05.879 --> 02:18:07.480
to re-emerge a bit.

02:18:07.480 --> 02:18:11.070
And partly, Vertov
is making a critique.

02:18:11.070 --> 02:18:14.980
I mean, you see some
people lying on benches

02:18:14.980 --> 02:18:17.549
and then you see other people
who look quite prosperous.

02:18:17.549 --> 02:18:19.709
So I think part of
what's going on there

02:18:19.709 --> 02:18:22.139
is making a critique,
Vertov making

02:18:22.139 --> 02:18:25.650
a critique with the new economic
policies during this period

02:18:25.650 --> 02:18:29.068
that you have a resurgence
of economic inequalities

02:18:29.068 --> 02:18:31.110
that would have been there
before the revolution.

02:18:31.110 --> 02:18:32.700
So he's also making
his own argument

02:18:32.700 --> 02:18:36.120
within the context of that
post-revolutionary period

02:18:36.120 --> 02:18:36.650
in Russia.

02:18:36.650 --> 02:18:39.450
So for those of you who haven't
done the background reading,

02:18:39.450 --> 02:18:41.575
that's something that would
have come out in there.

02:18:41.575 --> 02:18:45.840
But that's partly
what's also going on

02:18:45.840 --> 02:18:48.270
with those juxtapositions.

02:18:48.270 --> 02:18:51.629
But in general, then, the
sense that you get of people

02:18:51.629 --> 02:18:54.209
is a sense of
society in a broader

02:18:54.209 --> 02:18:59.184
sense, the relationships and
connections between people

02:18:59.184 --> 02:19:01.559
as part of Soviet society,
it's a collective image rather

02:19:01.559 --> 02:19:03.959
than being about individual
stories and narration.

02:19:03.959 --> 02:19:08.040
And again, reading
from the manifesto,

02:19:08.040 --> 02:19:10.350
Vertov writes, "We
temporarily exclude man

02:19:10.350 --> 02:19:13.700
as a subject for film.

02:19:13.700 --> 02:19:15.620
Our path leads
through the poetry

02:19:15.620 --> 02:19:18.469
of machines, from
the bungling citizen

02:19:18.469 --> 02:19:22.520
to the perfect electric man,
in revealing the machine soul,

02:19:22.520 --> 02:19:24.950
in causing the worker
to love his workbench,

02:19:24.950 --> 02:19:28.190
the peasant his tractor,
the engineer his engine,

02:19:28.190 --> 02:19:31.430
we introduce creative joy
into all mechanical labor.

02:19:31.430 --> 02:19:34.459
We bring people into closer
kinship with machines.

02:19:34.459 --> 02:19:35.629
We foster new people."

02:19:39.139 --> 02:19:42.340
So what's the kind of
image of society here?

02:19:44.976 --> 02:19:46.809
AUDIENCE: Each person
has a role in a bigger

02:19:46.809 --> 02:19:49.420
society which he's trying to
show how it all works together.

02:19:52.677 --> 02:19:54.260
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
And the relationship

02:19:54.260 --> 02:19:56.420
to technology here?

02:19:56.420 --> 02:19:58.640
AUDIENCE: Very much at MIT.

02:19:58.640 --> 02:19:59.990
We love the technology.

02:19:59.990 --> 02:20:01.860
That's what going
to save the world.

02:20:01.860 --> 02:20:06.330
We don't need politics, we
just need better iPhones.

02:20:06.330 --> 02:20:07.593
I mean, it's beautiful.

02:20:07.593 --> 02:20:10.010
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So it's very
much about people's kinship

02:20:10.010 --> 02:20:10.700
with machines.

02:20:10.700 --> 02:20:13.670
And again, a very
different image

02:20:13.670 --> 02:20:16.940
of modern technology
than you find in Flaherty

02:20:16.940 --> 02:20:19.190
just a few years apart.

02:20:19.190 --> 02:20:21.500
So you're seeing two
different trends,

02:20:21.500 --> 02:20:24.290
two different artistic
traditions happening

02:20:24.290 --> 02:20:26.400
not that far apart
chronologically,

02:20:26.400 --> 02:20:29.600
but this one being very
much about the celebration

02:20:29.600 --> 02:20:31.820
of modern technology
and where it can lead,

02:20:31.820 --> 02:20:34.542
and wanting to bring people into
closer kinship with machines,

02:20:34.542 --> 02:20:36.500
and what can we see
through these technologies,

02:20:36.500 --> 02:20:38.300
how does it let us
see the world anew.

02:20:38.300 --> 02:20:41.360
And in Flaherty, using the
newer technology, but also

02:20:41.360 --> 02:20:43.970
a critique of what he
would have been seeing

02:20:43.970 --> 02:20:49.310
is as modern social
life, looking

02:20:49.310 --> 02:20:53.658
at other kinds of technologies
with longer histories,

02:20:53.658 --> 02:20:55.700
again, in this kind of
romantic primitivist mode,

02:20:55.700 --> 02:20:59.540
but also a certain
amount of real respect

02:20:59.540 --> 02:21:01.260
for those other kinds
of technologies.

02:21:01.260 --> 02:21:07.340
So again, you've got these two
filmmakers, both incredibly

02:21:07.340 --> 02:21:10.190
influential
filmmakers, doing very

02:21:10.190 --> 02:21:12.060
interesting, fascinating,
innovative things,

02:21:12.060 --> 02:21:14.102
but coming from completely
different perspectives

02:21:14.102 --> 02:21:15.210
on these topics.

02:21:15.210 --> 02:21:15.770
AUDIENCE: Well,
you have to think,

02:21:15.770 --> 02:21:17.895
the reason it is in parallel
because they both have

02:21:17.895 --> 02:21:21.152
romantic representation, so
it's very much a representation

02:21:21.152 --> 02:21:21.860
of machines here.

02:21:21.860 --> 02:21:24.165
They're beautiful,
the mood is perfect.

02:21:24.165 --> 02:21:25.790
We don't really see
what these machines

02:21:25.790 --> 02:21:29.150
do when they break down.

02:21:29.150 --> 02:21:33.440
It's just about
as if they were--

02:21:33.440 --> 02:21:35.960
you can see this kind
of timeless world

02:21:35.960 --> 02:21:38.270
in the same way the people
are represented in Flaherty.

02:21:38.270 --> 02:21:40.430
So I think it's nice.

02:21:40.430 --> 02:21:43.640
CHRIS BOEBEL: And one
other thing about Vertov,

02:21:43.640 --> 02:21:46.040
at the beginning, he
very much describes

02:21:46.040 --> 02:21:47.510
the film as an experiment.

02:21:47.510 --> 02:21:49.520
I mean, the film
itself, film technology

02:21:49.520 --> 02:21:51.900
is one of the technologies
that he's talking about

02:21:51.900 --> 02:21:53.660
and that he's completely
obsessed with.

02:21:53.660 --> 02:21:59.090
And it's a scientific
or pseudoscientific

02:21:59.090 --> 02:22:00.093
representation.

02:22:00.093 --> 02:22:01.760
He doesn't call himself
a film director,

02:22:01.760 --> 02:22:04.460
he calls himself a chief
experimentalist or something

02:22:04.460 --> 02:22:05.280
like that.

02:22:05.280 --> 02:22:07.250
And the film itself
is an experiment.

02:22:07.250 --> 02:22:10.970
And that raises a very,
very interesting question

02:22:10.970 --> 02:22:14.930
that we're going to return to
periodically as we make films

02:22:14.930 --> 02:22:16.580
and look at films,
which is, what's

02:22:16.580 --> 02:22:19.370
the relationship
between documentaries,

02:22:19.370 --> 02:22:22.590
and documentary filmmaking,
and science itself?

02:22:22.590 --> 02:22:25.010
We're talking about
making science films.

02:22:25.010 --> 02:22:27.740
But when you make a
documentary, you go out,

02:22:27.740 --> 02:22:30.720
you collect evidence,
you collect data,

02:22:30.720 --> 02:22:33.770
you process the data
in various ways,

02:22:33.770 --> 02:22:36.050
and you come to some
kind of conclusion.

02:22:36.050 --> 02:22:37.888
And there are parallels.

02:22:37.888 --> 02:22:40.430
I mean, certainly it's a very
different process in many ways,

02:22:40.430 --> 02:22:42.860
but there are parallels
between that and science.

02:22:42.860 --> 02:22:44.270
And Vertov is saying, parallels?

02:22:44.270 --> 02:22:47.840
Hell, it's the same thing.

02:22:47.840 --> 02:22:51.310
But as we go on, we're
going to be looking

02:22:51.310 --> 02:22:55.480
at, what's the relationship
between those two processes

02:22:55.480 --> 02:22:59.410
and truth, reality, whatever
word you want to use

02:22:59.410 --> 02:23:01.730
to describe what we're after.

02:23:01.730 --> 02:23:03.880
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
So just to conclude,

02:23:03.880 --> 02:23:05.380
just to throw out
a couple of things

02:23:05.380 --> 02:23:08.777
to be thinking about then,
following what Chris is saying.

02:23:08.777 --> 02:23:10.360
As we go through the
rest of the class

02:23:10.360 --> 02:23:12.735
and as you think about making
final projects for yourself

02:23:12.735 --> 02:23:14.770
and different kind of
pieces, to thinking

02:23:14.770 --> 02:23:16.570
about the different
techniques you

02:23:16.570 --> 02:23:19.472
might be interested in using.

02:23:19.472 --> 02:23:21.430
I mean, what kind of
story do you want to tell?

02:23:21.430 --> 02:23:23.513
Do you want to tell a story
from the point of view

02:23:23.513 --> 02:23:26.260
of an individual and
follow him or her around?

02:23:26.260 --> 02:23:29.590
Do you want to use montage
to tell your story?

02:23:29.590 --> 02:23:31.285
Is the focus going
to be on editing

02:23:31.285 --> 02:23:33.910
as a way to construct the ideas
in the piece that you're doing?

02:23:33.910 --> 02:23:37.150
Do you want it to be more
narrative-driven or more

02:23:37.150 --> 02:23:38.950
non-narrative driven?

02:23:38.950 --> 02:23:43.298
Do you want it to be a realist
aesthetic where you're drawing

02:23:43.298 --> 02:23:44.590
attention away from the camera?

02:23:44.590 --> 02:23:46.330
Or this is highly reflexive.

02:23:46.330 --> 02:23:51.730
In some ways, the
Vertov piece is

02:23:51.730 --> 02:23:53.680
very contemporary in
the sense of being

02:23:53.680 --> 02:23:55.330
reflexive in the
sense of focusing

02:23:55.330 --> 02:23:57.450
on the process of
filmmaking itself as being

02:23:57.450 --> 02:23:58.450
part of what it's doing.

02:23:58.450 --> 02:24:00.790
Would you want to be
reflexive in your filmmaking?

02:24:00.790 --> 02:24:05.080
Would you include references
to yourself as the filmmaker?

02:24:05.080 --> 02:24:10.702
Shots of yourself talking
to people or your hand

02:24:10.702 --> 02:24:12.160
going out and doing
various things?

02:24:12.160 --> 02:24:13.790
Or I mean, do you want
to include yourself

02:24:13.790 --> 02:24:14.790
as part of this process?

02:24:20.340 --> 02:24:23.280
Do you, in relation to issues
of science and technology,

02:24:23.280 --> 02:24:25.890
do you want to
celebrate technology

02:24:25.890 --> 02:24:27.510
or are you interested
in exploring

02:24:27.510 --> 02:24:28.583
other kinds of issues?

02:24:28.583 --> 02:24:30.000
What kinds of
relationships do you

02:24:30.000 --> 02:24:32.083
want to foster with the
subjects that you're going

02:24:32.083 --> 02:24:33.480
to be portraying in your film?

02:24:33.480 --> 02:24:36.960
Do you want to have a
collaborative relationship

02:24:36.960 --> 02:24:39.037
that Flaherty had,
to some degree,

02:24:39.037 --> 02:24:40.620
even though it was
a hierarchical one,

02:24:40.620 --> 02:24:43.470
it was still based on a certain
degree of collaboration.

02:24:43.470 --> 02:24:45.000
Is that your
interest in creating

02:24:45.000 --> 02:24:46.875
that kind of relationship
with your subjects?

02:24:46.875 --> 02:24:49.160
Or are you interested
more in not so

02:24:49.160 --> 02:24:50.910
much the subject's
point of view, but more

02:24:50.910 --> 02:24:52.410
what you want to
say as a filmmaker,

02:24:52.410 --> 02:24:55.530
using those images that you take
to say to say something else?

02:24:55.530 --> 02:24:57.060
What kinds of
relationships do you

02:24:57.060 --> 02:25:00.360
want to foster in the
process of making films?

02:25:00.360 --> 02:25:01.927
Anything else I'm leaving out?

02:25:01.927 --> 02:25:02.760
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well--

02:25:02.760 --> 02:25:03.210
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
I'm sure a lot.

02:25:03.210 --> 02:25:04.835
CHRIS BOEBEL: A whole
semester's worth.

02:25:04.835 --> 02:25:05.800
Yeah, we'll keep going.

02:25:05.800 --> 02:25:07.508
CHRISTINE WALLEY: But
anyway, so the idea

02:25:07.508 --> 02:25:10.410
is to be thinking about these
completely-- two classic films

02:25:10.410 --> 02:25:13.140
that have been incredibly
influential and powerful,

02:25:13.140 --> 02:25:14.940
completely different
technique, styles,

02:25:14.940 --> 02:25:16.750
ways of editing,
everything else,

02:25:16.750 --> 02:25:19.290
and to think about the range
of choices that you all have.

02:25:19.290 --> 02:25:22.290
And when you go to think about
doing your own pieces, what

02:25:22.290 --> 02:25:24.240
interests you, what do
you want to drive you,

02:25:24.240 --> 02:25:28.530
what do you want to drive the
piece that you're going to do?

02:25:28.530 --> 02:25:30.420
CHRIS BOEBEL: So,
just the last word,

02:25:30.420 --> 02:25:33.210
just looking ahead to Friday.

02:25:33.210 --> 02:25:35.460
These are both silent films.

02:25:35.460 --> 02:25:38.490
On Friday, we're going
to introduce sound.

02:25:38.490 --> 02:25:40.240
You got to play with
the camera last week,

02:25:40.240 --> 02:25:42.600
so now we're going to
take the next step.

02:25:42.600 --> 02:25:45.210
And we'll also look at some
of the things that people shot

02:25:45.210 --> 02:25:48.590
and talk about what
worked and what didn't.

